WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:08.230
- So, hi, welcome, everyone.

00:00:08.230 --> 00:00:12.630
It's my distinct pleasure
to introduce Erick White,

00:00:12.630 --> 00:00:15.310
who I've known since
graduate school at Cornell,

00:00:15.310 --> 00:00:16.750
and who many of you know

00:00:16.750 --> 00:00:18.630
is one of the most distinguished scholars

00:00:18.630 --> 00:00:22.790
of Thai Buddhism and Southeast
Asian Buddhism generally

00:00:22.790 --> 00:00:25.870
working in North America right now.

00:00:25.870 --> 00:00:27.690
Erick is an independent scholar

00:00:27.690 --> 00:00:30.540
who has previously taught
at Antioch University,

00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:34.210
Cornell University, and
the University of Michigan.

00:00:34.210 --> 00:00:36.520
His research explores
the cultural politics

00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:39.750
of popular religion in
Thailand, the subculture

00:00:39.750 --> 00:00:40.770
and religious careers

00:00:40.770 --> 00:00:43.860
of Bangkok professional spirit mediums,

00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:45.560
and the social-cultural dynamics

00:00:45.560 --> 00:00:48.370
underlying claims to
authority and charisma

00:00:48.370 --> 00:00:50.180
in Theravada Buddhism.

00:00:50.180 --> 00:00:52.400
He's the author of numerous book chapters,

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:53.727
and journal articles including

00:00:53.727 --> 00:00:55.917
"Contemporary Buddhism, and Magic,"

00:00:55.917 --> 00:00:58.430
"The Cultural Politics of the Supernatural

00:00:58.430 --> 00:01:00.527
in Theravada Buddhist Thailand,"

00:01:00.527 --> 00:01:02.460
"Rethinking Vernacular Religion

00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:04.587
Across Mainland Southeast Asia,"

00:01:04.587 --> 00:01:07.760
"Rethinking Anthropological
Models of Spirit Possession

00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:09.580
and Theravada Buddhism,"

00:01:09.580 --> 00:01:12.650
and "Fraudulent And
Dangerous Popular Religiosity

00:01:12.650 --> 00:01:16.690
In The Public Sphere: Moral
Campaigns To Prohibit, Reform,

00:01:16.690 --> 00:01:19.850
And Demystify Thai Spirit Mediums."

00:01:19.850 --> 00:01:21.330
He's currently in the process

00:01:21.330 --> 00:01:24.407
of drafting a book manuscript entitled,

00:01:24.407 --> 00:01:27.810
"Spirit Possessions as Buddhist Vocation:

00:01:27.810 --> 00:01:31.620
Reimagining Piety In Modern Thailand."

00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:33.770
I think some of what
he'll be talking about

00:01:34.624 --> 00:01:36.540
will be engaging with
the topics of that book.

00:01:36.540 --> 00:01:40.207
So please join me in welcoming
Erick White for today's talk,

00:01:40.207 --> 00:01:43.810
"Spirit Possession In
Buddhist Southeast Asia."

00:01:43.810 --> 00:01:44.643
Erick.

00:01:46.472 --> 00:01:48.725
- Okay, thank you, Erik,
for that introduction.

00:01:48.725 --> 00:01:53.000
Thanks to the council for
inviting me to give a talk,

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:54.960
and for Chris managing the details,

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:57.650
and for everyone showing up digitally

00:01:57.650 --> 00:02:00.963
from wherever you are in
the world and time zone,

00:02:02.850 --> 00:02:07.733
I will share my screen.

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:13.720
So my talk today is actually about a book.

00:02:20.460 --> 00:02:23.370
It's not my book in progress,

00:02:23.370 --> 00:02:27.627
it's an edited volume that
just recently came out.

00:02:27.627 --> 00:02:29.910
"Spirit Possession In
Buddhist Southeast Asia

00:02:29.910 --> 00:02:31.910
Worlds Ever More Enchanted."

00:02:31.910 --> 00:02:35.363
I contributed the afterword to it.

00:02:39.190 --> 00:02:41.680
Basically, I do think
it's an important book,

00:02:41.680 --> 00:02:43.020
groundbreaking,

00:02:43.020 --> 00:02:45.740
I'll just say a few things
about initially why.

00:02:45.740 --> 00:02:49.230
The scholars have been
working on spirit possession

00:02:49.230 --> 00:02:52.930
in Buddhist Southeast
Asia for a long time,

00:02:52.930 --> 00:02:54.420
but this is the first book

00:02:54.420 --> 00:02:57.550
that has actually
brought scholars together

00:02:57.550 --> 00:02:58.550
who have been studying

00:02:58.550 --> 00:03:01.560
across different regions and countries

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:03.020
in Mainland Southeast Asia,

00:03:03.020 --> 00:03:05.730
and putting their
observation and arguments

00:03:05.730 --> 00:03:08.103
into conversation with each other.

00:03:09.220 --> 00:03:11.770
It brings together
scholars who have worked

00:03:11.770 --> 00:03:14.610
across the different
Theravada majority countries

00:03:14.610 --> 00:03:17.980
in Southeast Asia, that's unusual.

00:03:17.980 --> 00:03:22.520
It's also included in the
mix of scholarship work

00:03:22.520 --> 00:03:26.493
on spirit possession in Vietnam,
that's even more unusual.

00:03:27.381 --> 00:03:29.070
Work on spirit possession in Vietnam

00:03:29.070 --> 00:03:32.003
is tended to be siloed in many ways.

00:03:33.526 --> 00:03:36.140
And the book also brings together

00:03:36.140 --> 00:03:37.890
multiple generations of scholars.

00:03:37.890 --> 00:03:41.233
So the editors, Benedicte and Peter,

00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:44.460
they began working and writing
about spirit possession

00:03:44.460 --> 00:03:46.010
in the 1980s.

00:03:46.010 --> 00:03:49.830
and Irene Stengs and I began working,

00:03:49.830 --> 00:03:53.540
and writing on the topic in the 1990s,

00:03:53.540 --> 00:03:57.120
but the remaining eight
scholars in the book

00:03:57.120 --> 00:03:58.470
who contributed to the book

00:04:00.380 --> 00:04:03.790
began working in either
the 2000s, or the 2010s.

00:04:03.790 --> 00:04:07.650
So there really is a kind
of cross-generational cohort

00:04:07.650 --> 00:04:09.610
sort of conversation going on in the book,

00:04:09.610 --> 00:04:10.663
and that's unusual.

00:04:11.720 --> 00:04:14.140
Basically, as you can see
from the table of contents,

00:04:14.140 --> 00:04:16.990
there's like 10 case studies,

00:04:16.990 --> 00:04:21.523
and then five out of work in Thailand,

00:04:24.220 --> 00:04:27.230
two out of work in Myanmar,
two out of work in Cambodia,

00:04:27.230 --> 00:04:29.680
one from Vietnam.

00:04:29.680 --> 00:04:32.100
It's too bad there's
not more from Vietnam,

00:04:32.100 --> 00:04:35.260
but there's sort of size limitations.

00:04:35.260 --> 00:04:37.611
And then there's an introduction,
and then afterwords,

00:04:37.611 --> 00:04:39.800
and both the introduction,
and the afterwords

00:04:39.800 --> 00:04:41.130
in a sense

00:04:41.130 --> 00:04:44.410
take the measure of the
different contributions,

00:04:44.410 --> 00:04:47.860
and place them in terms of
the existing scholarship,

00:04:47.860 --> 00:04:50.503
and then point towards pass forward.

00:04:55.560 --> 00:04:58.000
My talk today is about the
book and its importance,

00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:02.840
and the contribution it makes
to the ongoing conversation,

00:05:02.840 --> 00:05:03.760
but there's some caveats.

00:05:03.760 --> 00:05:08.760
My talk isn't simply a
recapitulation of the afterward,

00:05:08.940 --> 00:05:11.410
and it's not a detailed
or exhausted discussion

00:05:11.410 --> 00:05:14.078
of the book's content and
its particular chapters.

00:05:14.078 --> 00:05:17.260
I wanna sketch a broader in general terms,

00:05:17.260 --> 00:05:19.490
the intellectual value of the book,

00:05:19.490 --> 00:05:22.990
and to make sense of it
and its contributions.

00:05:22.990 --> 00:05:25.620
I wanna place the book in a larger,

00:05:25.620 --> 00:05:27.860
and longer intellectual history

00:05:27.860 --> 00:05:29.630
of the study of spirit possession

00:05:29.630 --> 00:05:30.810
in Buddhist Southeast Asia,

00:05:30.810 --> 00:05:34.820
and see the book as contributing
to an ongoing conversation,

00:05:34.820 --> 00:05:38.076
and debate that's happened
across scholarly generations.

00:05:38.076 --> 00:05:42.320
And as you'll see, I place
the work in this volume

00:05:42.320 --> 00:05:44.050
in sort of what I term

00:05:44.050 --> 00:05:46.513
the third generation of
scholarship on the topic.

00:05:47.920 --> 00:05:50.237
And basically I'm gonna be talking about

00:05:50.237 --> 00:05:55.237
the way empirical foci of
studies, the analytics, themes,

00:05:55.880 --> 00:05:57.870
and the theoretical
conceptions have shifted

00:05:57.870 --> 00:05:59.612
across those generations

00:05:59.612 --> 00:06:04.270
in a kind of cumulative evolving way,

00:06:04.270 --> 00:06:06.810
but I'm not suggesting
that later generations

00:06:06.810 --> 00:06:11.810
simply replace the prior
generations topics and themes

00:06:13.000 --> 00:06:16.840
and analyses, but sort of
layered and supplemented,

00:06:16.840 --> 00:06:19.813
and sometimes with
agreement and disagreement.

00:06:22.340 --> 00:06:25.600
The other qualification is I'm
leaving out for the most part

00:06:25.600 --> 00:06:29.053
detailed bibliographic
and citational references.

00:06:31.030 --> 00:06:34.110
I've mapped these scholarly
generational cohorts

00:06:34.110 --> 00:06:38.920
in these sort of 20 year groupings,

00:06:38.920 --> 00:06:40.920
which is obviously kind of artificial,

00:06:40.920 --> 00:06:43.690
and I paint with a
necessarily broad brush.

00:06:43.690 --> 00:06:45.870
So there are obvious exceptions

00:06:45.870 --> 00:06:47.596
to some of the claims I'm making.

00:06:47.596 --> 00:06:49.620
When I talk about spirit possession

00:06:49.620 --> 00:06:53.110
in Buddhist Southeast Asia,
I'm talking about Mainland.

00:06:53.110 --> 00:06:58.110
So I exclude Buddhism in
insular Southeast Asia,

00:06:58.500 --> 00:07:00.530
and I also exclude discussions about

00:07:00.530 --> 00:07:02.550
the geographic borderlands

00:07:02.550 --> 00:07:04.330
of Mainland Buddhist Southeast Asia,

00:07:04.330 --> 00:07:07.860
like Northern Malaysia, Southern China,

00:07:07.860 --> 00:07:10.636
Bangladesh, even India.

00:07:10.636 --> 00:07:12.360
And the other thing

00:07:12.360 --> 00:07:14.572
is I'm not talking about spirit possession

00:07:14.572 --> 00:07:17.180
in upland societies and cultures,

00:07:17.180 --> 00:07:20.023
I'm talking about it in
lowland societies and cultures.

00:07:21.395 --> 00:07:24.700
The conversation and debates
are a whole different beast

00:07:24.700 --> 00:07:26.723
in terms of the uplands.

00:07:27.900 --> 00:07:30.050
And there's likely a Thai-centric bias

00:07:30.050 --> 00:07:32.750
to the kind of intellectual
history I'm going to give.

00:07:34.210 --> 00:07:35.750
My discussion is mostly probably rooted

00:07:35.750 --> 00:07:38.573
in English language scholarship.

00:07:40.380 --> 00:07:45.180
So unlike my afterword,

00:07:45.180 --> 00:07:46.260
I'm actually gonna talk a little bit

00:07:46.260 --> 00:07:49.480
about the pre-history of the
study of spirit possession

00:07:49.480 --> 00:07:51.520
in Buddhist Southeast Asia,

00:07:51.520 --> 00:07:53.820
which is to say, there's
this first generation

00:07:53.820 --> 00:07:57.112
that I see as starting
in around from 1965,

00:07:57.112 --> 00:08:02.112
but the way spirit possession
and Buddhism was thought about

00:08:03.070 --> 00:08:05.650
before this time period is important

00:08:05.650 --> 00:08:06.960
for understanding what happens later.

00:08:06.960 --> 00:08:08.330
It kind of haunts,

00:08:08.330 --> 00:08:10.900
and even shapes the
kinds of conversations.

00:08:10.900 --> 00:08:13.400
There's sort of foundational ideas

00:08:13.400 --> 00:08:15.793
that are sort of laid down in dilemmas,

00:08:15.793 --> 00:08:18.160
and they continue to reverberate.

00:08:18.160 --> 00:08:21.691
And you'll see that's
part of the discussion

00:08:21.691 --> 00:08:25.193
that I'm presenting to you.

00:08:26.070 --> 00:08:28.230
I've tried to give
little descriptive tropes

00:08:28.230 --> 00:08:31.150
for each of these sort of generations.

00:08:31.150 --> 00:08:33.559
And in the case of the prehistory,

00:08:33.559 --> 00:08:38.220
I talk about spirit possession
ignored or marginalized.

00:08:38.220 --> 00:08:41.193
So, basically, in this earliest period,

00:08:45.750 --> 00:08:48.100
spirit possession in general

00:08:48.100 --> 00:08:50.920
has always been stigmatized
within both colonial

00:08:50.920 --> 00:08:53.430
and post-colonial scholarship on religion.

00:08:53.430 --> 00:08:58.040
It is seen as a remnant of the historical,

00:08:58.040 --> 00:09:00.070
and civilizational precursors

00:09:00.070 --> 00:09:02.500
to the world of historical religions.

00:09:02.500 --> 00:09:05.720
People don't talk about spirit
mediums and spirit possession

00:09:05.720 --> 00:09:10.563
being central to the vision
of the ethical good life,

00:09:14.360 --> 00:09:17.032
and the practices and
how to gain transcendence

00:09:17.032 --> 00:09:22.032
if you're a Christian or
a Muslim or a Buddhist,

00:09:22.130 --> 00:09:24.020
or Judaism, or what-not,

00:09:24.020 --> 00:09:26.293
in that sense spirit possession

00:09:26.293 --> 00:09:31.293
has always been located as
a kind of relic, or survival

00:09:31.640 --> 00:09:35.370
of a pre-world historical
religious imaginary.

00:09:35.370 --> 00:09:38.830
And you can see that in many
common semantic associations

00:09:38.830 --> 00:09:40.473
of spirit possession.

00:09:40.473 --> 00:09:43.800
Shamanism, magic, divination,

00:09:43.800 --> 00:09:46.310
superstition, animism, spirit cults,

00:09:46.310 --> 00:09:49.300
these are all terms that
are circulated around it,

00:09:49.300 --> 00:09:52.861
or sometimes can serve as replacements,

00:09:52.861 --> 00:09:55.390
and they all sort of point

00:09:55.390 --> 00:10:00.180
to a pre-world historic
religious imaginary,

00:10:00.180 --> 00:10:01.630
and oftentimes spirit possession I think,

00:10:01.630 --> 00:10:06.630
is seen as a kind of descendant,
or stepchild of Shamanism,

00:10:06.891 --> 00:10:09.130
but this has created

00:10:09.130 --> 00:10:11.240
a kind of foundational conceptual dilemma.

00:10:11.240 --> 00:10:13.730
Spirit possession is always constituted

00:10:13.730 --> 00:10:16.450
as radically other to and outside world

00:10:16.450 --> 00:10:18.150
historical religions,

00:10:18.150 --> 00:10:21.600
and this historical and
civilizational framing creates

00:10:21.600 --> 00:10:22.810
this enduring dilemma

00:10:22.810 --> 00:10:24.940
about how to understand the relationship

00:10:24.940 --> 00:10:28.490
between spirit possession and
world historical religions.

00:10:28.490 --> 00:10:30.610
Spirit possession tends to always look

00:10:30.610 --> 00:10:32.110
like something illicit

00:10:32.110 --> 00:10:34.470
which doesn't belong in a world religion,

00:10:34.470 --> 00:10:37.720
but has been surreptitiously
smuggled into it.

00:10:37.720 --> 00:10:40.280
And for scholars of
Southeast Asian Buddhism,

00:10:40.280 --> 00:10:42.958
they've tended to conceptualize
this relationship,

00:10:42.958 --> 00:10:45.340
and this belonging and this smuggling

00:10:45.340 --> 00:10:48.170
through the lens of
this idea of syncretism,

00:10:48.170 --> 00:10:50.313
which I will talk about along the way.

00:10:52.230 --> 00:10:56.433
So you can see in this
earliest period of scholarship,

00:10:57.550 --> 00:11:00.072
spirit possession has been
ignored and marginalized

00:11:00.072 --> 00:11:01.840
in the study of Southeast Asian Buddhism

00:11:01.840 --> 00:11:05.200
'cause scholars are
working on documenting,

00:11:05.200 --> 00:11:08.200
and studying things that
were seen as more canonical,

00:11:08.200 --> 00:11:10.163
widespread, and mainstream,

00:11:11.245 --> 00:11:13.080
and spirit mediums and possession

00:11:13.080 --> 00:11:15.950
didn't seem to have any
relation to these, right?

00:11:15.950 --> 00:11:18.250
So making sense of monks and laity,

00:11:18.250 --> 00:11:20.150
and their relations to each other,

00:11:20.150 --> 00:11:22.910
making sense of monasteries
and temple life,

00:11:22.910 --> 00:11:25.430
what is the Sangha, what is monastic life,

00:11:25.430 --> 00:11:27.980
what's the renunciatory path.

00:11:27.980 --> 00:11:30.400
Different kinds of rituals
and actions like meditation,

00:11:30.400 --> 00:11:33.625
and merit-making and ritual cycles.

00:11:33.625 --> 00:11:37.940
How to understand cosmology
and karma and rebirth.

00:11:37.940 --> 00:11:41.530
How to understand Nirvana
and practical ethics,

00:11:41.530 --> 00:11:43.410
and what the good life is.

00:11:43.410 --> 00:11:48.020
For most scholars,
especially in the colonial,

00:11:48.020 --> 00:11:51.170
post-colonial era, spirit
possession and spirit mediums

00:11:52.322 --> 00:11:54.000
seem irrelevant to sort
of really making sense

00:11:54.000 --> 00:11:55.270
of all of that.

00:11:55.270 --> 00:11:58.541
So you can basically see
that sort of Buddhologists,

00:11:58.541 --> 00:11:59.670
and religious studies scholars

00:11:59.670 --> 00:12:02.433
who work with primarily,

00:12:02.433 --> 00:12:04.960
sort of canonical literary sources

00:12:04.960 --> 00:12:07.343
in Southeast Asia of Theravada Buddhism.

00:12:09.510 --> 00:12:12.640
Those sources are silent for the most part

00:12:12.640 --> 00:12:14.360
about spirit possession
and spirit mediums,

00:12:14.360 --> 00:12:16.330
or they have negative
representations of them

00:12:16.330 --> 00:12:19.080
in terms of malevolence
and inauspiciousness,

00:12:19.080 --> 00:12:22.910
and beastly arts and things
that need to be exorcized.

00:12:22.910 --> 00:12:25.700
And there's also no real
obvious institutional presence,

00:12:25.700 --> 00:12:28.740
or significance of spirit
possession in the region,

00:12:28.740 --> 00:12:30.110
which is different than Tibetan Buddhism,

00:12:30.110 --> 00:12:31.650
which has State Oracles, right?

00:12:31.650 --> 00:12:34.792
You have highly accomplished monastics

00:12:34.792 --> 00:12:39.792
who are possessed by guardian deities,

00:12:40.280 --> 00:12:43.740
and then the Dali Lama, and other figures

00:12:43.740 --> 00:12:47.040
consult with these
possessed beings, right?

00:12:47.040 --> 00:12:49.590
So there's nothing like State Oracles

00:12:49.590 --> 00:12:53.757
in the Southeast Asian Buddhist tradition.

00:12:53.757 --> 00:12:58.240
It's been marginalized by
historians and anthropologists.

00:12:58.240 --> 00:13:01.750
There aren't many historical
references to spirit possession

00:13:01.750 --> 00:13:02.892
in archival sources.

00:13:02.892 --> 00:13:05.220
Their voices weren't recorded.

00:13:05.220 --> 00:13:09.703
They didn't produce records
that were preserved.

00:13:10.620 --> 00:13:15.620
Anthropologists have been
the ones who have recognized,

00:13:16.060 --> 00:13:18.360
and focused on spirit possession the most.

00:13:18.360 --> 00:13:20.250
And it makes frequent appearances

00:13:20.250 --> 00:13:22.160
in anthropological studies,

00:13:22.160 --> 00:13:23.656
but usually it's relatively brief

00:13:23.656 --> 00:13:25.980
because they themselves, anthropologists,

00:13:25.980 --> 00:13:29.420
are focusing on those same
kind of more mainstream

00:13:29.420 --> 00:13:32.220
conventional sorts of topics.

00:13:32.220 --> 00:13:36.370
And this is even true in later
generations of scholarship

00:13:36.370 --> 00:13:37.780
after 1965.

00:13:37.780 --> 00:13:40.075
There's an explosion of ethnographies

00:13:40.075 --> 00:13:44.420
written by anthropologists about
Buddhism in Southeast Asia,

00:13:44.420 --> 00:13:47.000
and usually discussions
of spirit possessions

00:13:47.000 --> 00:13:50.270
sort of show up in a paragraph or two,

00:13:50.270 --> 00:13:51.973
or some footnotes, or what-not.

00:13:53.514 --> 00:13:56.170
The first kind of exceptions of this

00:13:56.170 --> 00:13:57.350
came with anthropologists,

00:13:57.350 --> 00:13:59.932
and anthropologists have been
the majority of the scholars

00:13:59.932 --> 00:14:03.933
looking at spirit possession
in Southeast Asia.

00:14:05.540 --> 00:14:08.060
There was an exception to the rule

00:14:08.060 --> 00:14:09.557
in terms of even anthropologists.

00:14:09.557 --> 00:14:12.976
And I see this as this first
generation of scholarship.

00:14:12.976 --> 00:14:17.700
And I see it in this
era from 1965 to 1985.

00:14:17.700 --> 00:14:21.670
And my trope is they
think of spirit possession

00:14:21.670 --> 00:14:26.670
as this domesticated niche
within Buddhist Southeast Asia.

00:14:26.860 --> 00:14:31.000
So there's basically three scholars.

00:14:31.000 --> 00:14:35.010
Melford Spiro, who looked at possession,

00:14:35.010 --> 00:14:37.823
and the Nat cults in Burma,

00:14:38.855 --> 00:14:43.855
and Stanley Tambiah, who worked
on and studied possession

00:14:44.250 --> 00:14:47.610
in the context of spirit
cults in Northeast Thailand,

00:14:47.610 --> 00:14:51.740
and Thomas Kirsch who
worked on spirit possession

00:14:53.420 --> 00:14:57.853
and sort of the Buddhist order
also in Northeast Thailand.

00:14:58.900 --> 00:15:00.450
And there's not a lot of other scholars

00:15:00.450 --> 00:15:04.840
who really sort of focus
in with much more depth

00:15:04.840 --> 00:15:09.058
in terms of spirit possession
during this period,

00:15:09.058 --> 00:15:12.150
but these are sort of primary sources

00:15:12.150 --> 00:15:15.270
that people keep going back
to for reference points

00:15:15.270 --> 00:15:17.575
in later generations.

00:15:17.575 --> 00:15:22.575
So, basically, they've been
working in Myanmar and Thailand.

00:15:22.680 --> 00:15:25.074
These seminal pieces or scholarship

00:15:25.074 --> 00:15:26.860
aren't really centered in Laos.

00:15:26.860 --> 00:15:28.810
Although, you can think
about Northeast Thailand

00:15:28.810 --> 00:15:32.460
as sort of an ethnic
Lao sort of borderland

00:15:32.460 --> 00:15:35.693
between Siam, or Thailand and Lao,

00:15:35.693 --> 00:15:38.493
nobody is really from Cambodia.

00:15:42.010 --> 00:15:45.000
These scholars focused on certain
empirical sorts of things.

00:15:45.000 --> 00:15:46.730
They spent a lot of time elaborating

00:15:46.730 --> 00:15:50.600
the ties of spirit possession
to pre-Buddhist religiosity,

00:15:50.600 --> 00:15:53.783
and animism, and/or the spirit cults.

00:15:54.700 --> 00:15:57.843
They saw spirit possession
as strongly emblematic

00:15:57.843 --> 00:16:01.430
of the local folk religiosity found

00:16:01.430 --> 00:16:05.600
in rural peasant society and culture.

00:16:05.600 --> 00:16:08.730
They spent a decent amount of
time thinking about trance,

00:16:08.730 --> 00:16:12.530
and how that related to other
kinds of ritual experiences,

00:16:12.530 --> 00:16:15.710
but also looking at the ritual process

00:16:15.710 --> 00:16:19.560
that spirit mediums took part
in and how it played out,

00:16:19.560 --> 00:16:22.733
and the way it was part
of a ritual healing.

00:16:23.850 --> 00:16:25.040
Spiro actually said,

00:16:25.040 --> 00:16:26.750
his book is called
"Burmese Supernaturalism."

00:16:26.750 --> 00:16:31.065
He said he could have titled
it instead, "Burmese Medicine,"

00:16:31.065 --> 00:16:35.193
that's how closely he saw
these kinds of topics.

00:16:36.040 --> 00:16:38.807
They all tend to look at spirit possession

00:16:38.807 --> 00:16:41.223
and spirit mediums as providing

00:16:41.223 --> 00:16:43.780
this narrow set of
mundane worldly benefits

00:16:43.780 --> 00:16:44.890
that were really not...

00:16:44.890 --> 00:16:45.880
They didn't see as provided

00:16:45.880 --> 00:16:48.210
by Orthodox Buddhist actors
or activities, right?

00:16:48.210 --> 00:16:51.650
So it's not just that you
don't turn to a spirit medium

00:16:51.650 --> 00:16:55.870
for help in terms of gaining
liberation from Samsara,

00:16:55.870 --> 00:16:58.130
or in terms of a better rebirth,

00:16:58.130 --> 00:16:59.777
or even in terms of Samsara

00:17:01.361 --> 00:17:02.940
like the kind of benefits you
could get from merit-making.

00:17:02.940 --> 00:17:05.850
Instead it's narrower kinds of things

00:17:05.850 --> 00:17:08.170
in terms of local concerns, right?

00:17:08.170 --> 00:17:12.770
Violating norms about
belonging in kinship groups,

00:17:12.770 --> 00:17:14.806
or in, like, village communities,

00:17:14.806 --> 00:17:18.585
or sexual ethoses, and rules and whatnot,

00:17:18.585 --> 00:17:22.490
or healing of particular
kinds of illnesses

00:17:22.490 --> 00:17:27.160
that have been brought
about by maybe affliction

00:17:27.160 --> 00:17:28.940
by malevolent beings and whatnot.

00:17:28.940 --> 00:17:31.240
And the other thing they all talk about

00:17:31.240 --> 00:17:34.550
is how these spirit possession

00:17:34.550 --> 00:17:37.580
provides a sort of a stage

00:17:37.580 --> 00:17:39.430
in which there's a kind of sanctioned

00:17:39.430 --> 00:17:42.230
bacchanalian excess and pleasure,

00:17:42.230 --> 00:17:44.110
and that this has a certain...

00:17:44.110 --> 00:17:47.800
It's like a legitimate kind of violation

00:17:47.800 --> 00:17:49.450
of conventional Buddhist norms

00:17:49.450 --> 00:17:54.450
in terms of drinking and revelry,
and all this other stuff.

00:17:56.850 --> 00:17:59.950
And they all, in various degrees,

00:17:59.950 --> 00:18:04.950
map out how spirit mediums
are at one end of a pole

00:18:06.190 --> 00:18:09.250
of a kind of segregated
structural opposition

00:18:09.250 --> 00:18:11.960
from spirit mediums to Buddhist monks.

00:18:11.960 --> 00:18:13.090
And they look at it

00:18:13.090 --> 00:18:16.286
in terms of like, the kinds
of rituals they engage in,

00:18:16.286 --> 00:18:21.286
the behavioral ethos, even their
presence on a ritual stage.

00:18:22.480 --> 00:18:26.863
They will talk about
how Buddhist monks exit

00:18:26.863 --> 00:18:30.300
then later spirit mediums will enter,

00:18:30.300 --> 00:18:33.900
even if it's a kind of
conjoint ritual in some sense,

00:18:33.900 --> 00:18:37.280
they're almost spatially
and temporarily segregated

00:18:37.280 --> 00:18:38.113
in many ways,

00:18:38.113 --> 00:18:41.183
and there's a reticence in
their kind of engagement.

00:18:42.690 --> 00:18:45.190
Analytically, these scholars
are spending a lot of time

00:18:45.190 --> 00:18:46.780
trying to make sense

00:18:46.780 --> 00:18:49.790
of the character and
dynamics of Syncretism

00:18:49.790 --> 00:18:51.640
which is related

00:18:51.640 --> 00:18:55.120
and how these different
pieces sort of fit together,

00:18:55.120 --> 00:18:58.510
how spirit mediums and spirit possession

00:18:58.510 --> 00:19:01.480
sort of sits inside of a world

00:19:01.480 --> 00:19:05.443
structured and dominated by
Buddhist values and principles.

00:19:07.450 --> 00:19:10.170
They're also all to some degree
working with this question

00:19:10.170 --> 00:19:12.150
of the great tradition
and the little tradition,

00:19:12.150 --> 00:19:14.870
and how Buddhism as a world religion

00:19:14.870 --> 00:19:18.020
has been localized in the region,

00:19:18.020 --> 00:19:21.510
but also within agricultural communities.

00:19:21.510 --> 00:19:25.190
They're all sort of trying to
map the total religious field

00:19:25.190 --> 00:19:27.460
of these rural peasant villages

00:19:27.460 --> 00:19:28.993
where they did their research.

00:19:30.417 --> 00:19:33.957
Tambiah has this sort of famous map,

00:19:35.830 --> 00:19:38.860
sort a four part map of
these different religious

00:19:38.860 --> 00:19:41.270
sort of virtuosos, monks and mediums,

00:19:41.270 --> 00:19:45.480
and the masters of vital
essences and exorcists,

00:19:45.480 --> 00:19:49.530
and the different kinds of
rituals and performative,

00:19:49.530 --> 00:19:54.460
and even material that
sort of spread out of that.

00:19:54.460 --> 00:19:59.168
Kirsch tends to talk about
Buddhist and Brahmanical,

00:19:59.168 --> 00:20:04.168
and animistic sort of divisions
in the religious field,

00:20:04.300 --> 00:20:06.865
and how they're related
to each other, right?

00:20:06.865 --> 00:20:10.145
And they're all sort of...

00:20:10.145 --> 00:20:12.260
To a degree they focus on spirit mediums

00:20:12.260 --> 00:20:13.588
and spirit possession,

00:20:13.588 --> 00:20:16.548
in order to illuminate this larger puzzle

00:20:16.548 --> 00:20:18.780
of how Syncretism works,
how localization works,

00:20:18.780 --> 00:20:20.953
how the religious field works.

00:20:22.080 --> 00:20:25.080
So their discussions of spirit
possession and spirit mediums

00:20:28.440 --> 00:20:29.583
roots into that.

00:20:30.860 --> 00:20:32.330
And they all approach these

00:20:32.330 --> 00:20:34.460
from different kinds of perspectives

00:20:35.440 --> 00:20:37.283
in the anthropological tradition.

00:20:39.130 --> 00:20:41.340
Spiro has a kind of functionalist,

00:20:41.340 --> 00:20:43.690
and he's a psychological, anthropologist

00:20:43.690 --> 00:20:48.300
informed by Freudian kinds of thinking.

00:20:48.300 --> 00:20:52.830
Tambiah is sort of more of a
British social anthropologist

00:20:52.830 --> 00:20:55.010
informed by structuralist kinds of models.

00:20:55.010 --> 00:21:00.010
Kirsch is an American trained
who's been formed by Parsons,

00:21:00.720 --> 00:21:03.512
and his vision of sort of sociological,

00:21:03.512 --> 00:21:05.843
sort of dynamics and structure.

00:21:06.815 --> 00:21:11.815
And the way they conceptualize
spirit possession

00:21:12.090 --> 00:21:13.670
in Southeast Asian Buddhism,

00:21:13.670 --> 00:21:15.230
they tend to talk about it

00:21:15.230 --> 00:21:17.167
in terms of like I said, syncretism.

00:21:17.167 --> 00:21:20.080
Kirsch uses this language
of inclusive syncretism.

00:21:20.080 --> 00:21:23.220
I've sort of tended to
add hegemonic on to that.

00:21:23.220 --> 00:21:25.010
So spirit possession

00:21:25.010 --> 00:21:28.010
is seen as hegemonically
dominated by Buddhism.

00:21:28.010 --> 00:21:30.731
It's domesticated and constrained

00:21:30.731 --> 00:21:34.640
in its practices and actions and scope

00:21:34.640 --> 00:21:37.030
by Buddhist actors, and institutions,

00:21:37.030 --> 00:21:38.789
and beliefs and practices.

00:21:38.789 --> 00:21:42.020
There's even a sense,
especially from Kirsch

00:21:42.020 --> 00:21:44.850
that historically spirit
possession has been...

00:21:44.850 --> 00:21:48.370
If it is this remnants of a
pre-Buddhist kind of worldview,

00:21:48.370 --> 00:21:51.550
it's been reconfigured over time,

00:21:51.550 --> 00:21:53.650
and even fragmented in certain ways

00:21:53.650 --> 00:21:55.550
so that it is ultimately subordinate

00:21:55.550 --> 00:21:58.650
to Buddhist values, and goals
and frames of references.

00:21:58.650 --> 00:22:02.590
And spirit possession,
therefore, is seen as essentially

00:22:02.590 --> 00:22:05.200
kind of peripheral to
the normative mainstream

00:22:05.200 --> 00:22:07.077
Orthodox Buddhist order,

00:22:07.077 --> 00:22:09.904
and it provides these specialized niche

00:22:09.904 --> 00:22:13.820
of unique, but relatively
marginal services,

00:22:13.820 --> 00:22:16.110
activities, experience, and functions

00:22:16.110 --> 00:22:20.240
for rural village Southeast Asians

00:22:20.240 --> 00:22:22.600
when looked at from the frame

00:22:22.600 --> 00:22:25.000
of what a Buddhist good life is,

00:22:25.000 --> 00:22:27.560
and what moral practice is,

00:22:27.560 --> 00:22:30.540
and ritual practices one should engage in.

00:22:30.540 --> 00:22:31.860
And I just wanna emphasize here

00:22:31.860 --> 00:22:36.760
these terms of like dominated,
constrained, subordinated,

00:22:36.760 --> 00:22:38.640
peripheral marginal,

00:22:38.640 --> 00:22:41.210
because not to sort of ruin the plot line

00:22:41.210 --> 00:22:42.940
of the rest of the talk,

00:22:42.940 --> 00:22:47.320
but you could see the later generations

00:22:47.320 --> 00:22:52.320
in different ways, essentially
challenging this language,

00:22:53.574 --> 00:22:57.860
qualifying it, maybe
pushing back even more

00:22:57.860 --> 00:23:02.860
in terms of dismantling
it, or disagreeing with it,

00:23:02.950 --> 00:23:07.726
displacing it, or even
inverting these kinds of claims

00:23:07.726 --> 00:23:12.726
as they encounter spirit possession

00:23:13.860 --> 00:23:16.200
in Buddhist Southeast Asia.

00:23:16.200 --> 00:23:19.913
And the thing to keep in mind
is that the later generations

00:23:22.570 --> 00:23:26.060
are encountering a different...

00:23:26.060 --> 00:23:27.370
Spirit possession itself,

00:23:27.370 --> 00:23:31.500
and spirit mediums are
changing in subsequent decades.

00:23:31.500 --> 00:23:33.750
So the scholars are encountering

00:23:36.910 --> 00:23:38.930
transformed visions of spirit possession,

00:23:38.930 --> 00:23:43.150
and spirit mediumship, and
also they're encountering...

00:23:45.406 --> 00:23:47.370
It's a totally transformed
kind of Buddhist world,

00:23:47.370 --> 00:23:49.203
and broader social world as well.

00:23:51.910 --> 00:23:54.220
The second generation I
see is this 20 year period

00:23:54.220 --> 00:23:56.890
from 1985 to 2005,

00:23:56.890 --> 00:23:59.324
and this is when basically
you're thinking about

00:23:59.324 --> 00:24:01.700
the trope is spirit possession

00:24:01.700 --> 00:24:06.680
as religious innovation within
Buddhist Southeast Asia.

00:24:06.680 --> 00:24:11.680
So, Myanmar as a site of
study of spirit possession

00:24:12.290 --> 00:24:13.930
in Buddhist Southeast Asia

00:24:13.930 --> 00:24:17.577
fades in this era because anthropologists,

00:24:17.577 --> 00:24:21.607
the primary people
studying this phenomenon,

00:24:21.607 --> 00:24:23.610
obviously, for political reasons

00:24:23.610 --> 00:24:26.010
have found it difficult
to get into the country.

00:24:26.960 --> 00:24:30.000
And the one exception is
Benedict Brac de la Perriere

00:24:30.000 --> 00:24:33.960
who wrote a book in many articles
on the cult of the 37 Nats

00:24:33.960 --> 00:24:37.890
following in Spiro's footprints,

00:24:37.890 --> 00:24:41.703
but other than that work sort of dwindles.

00:24:42.760 --> 00:24:43.950
There's some historical work.

00:24:43.950 --> 00:24:46.760
So the most the scholarship in this period

00:24:46.760 --> 00:24:48.873
comes out of Thailand, frankly.

00:24:50.720 --> 00:24:52.370
The topic still isn't on the horizons

00:24:52.370 --> 00:24:54.970
for people working in Laos
or Cambodia the same way.

00:24:56.660 --> 00:24:59.810
These studies are strongly
shaped by this intensifying

00:24:59.810 --> 00:25:01.920
post World War II
transformation of Thailand.

00:25:01.920 --> 00:25:04.520
It began before this period, right?

00:25:04.520 --> 00:25:07.593
In the '60s, and then
essentially the '70s.

00:25:07.593 --> 00:25:10.720
You have extensive rural urban migration,

00:25:10.720 --> 00:25:14.010
exploding urbanization, a
rapid growth in education,

00:25:14.010 --> 00:25:15.970
especially higher education,

00:25:15.970 --> 00:25:18.480
the intensification of
capitalist development,

00:25:18.480 --> 00:25:21.932
the growth of a professional middle class,

00:25:21.932 --> 00:25:26.120
deepening democratization and
expansion of civil society,

00:25:26.120 --> 00:25:29.750
burgeoning mass media,
intensifying nationalism,

00:25:29.750 --> 00:25:32.240
and even the revival of the
monarchy, and all of these,

00:25:32.240 --> 00:25:33.370
I won't go into the details,

00:25:33.370 --> 00:25:34.732
but they also are fit into...

00:25:34.732 --> 00:25:35.910
These are the forces

00:25:35.910 --> 00:25:38.450
that are transforming spirit possession,

00:25:38.450 --> 00:25:40.583
and spirit mediumship on the ground.

00:25:42.340 --> 00:25:45.730
So, in terms of new foci that come up,

00:25:45.730 --> 00:25:47.610
all of a sudden you see
a lot of these studies

00:25:47.610 --> 00:25:51.450
talk about spirit mediums in cities,

00:25:51.450 --> 00:25:53.370
the discovery of spirit mediums in cities,

00:25:53.370 --> 00:25:57.303
like Chiang Mai, and Bangkol,
and Khon Khaen in particular.

00:26:00.250 --> 00:26:01.120
Prior generations

00:26:01.120 --> 00:26:03.640
didn't talk about spirit
mediums in cities.

00:26:03.640 --> 00:26:07.210
These new generation
also really digs into...

00:26:07.210 --> 00:26:09.790
The other thing about their scholarship

00:26:09.790 --> 00:26:13.827
is unlike Spiro, Tambiah, and Kirsch,

00:26:13.827 --> 00:26:17.870
their publications are
focused sort of exclusively

00:26:17.870 --> 00:26:20.860
on spirit mediums and going into detail

00:26:20.860 --> 00:26:23.220
about mediums and possession.

00:26:23.220 --> 00:26:25.200
A lot of articles, no books.

00:26:25.200 --> 00:26:28.930
So they go into unpacking the biographies

00:26:28.930 --> 00:26:30.950
of individual spirit mediums,

00:26:30.950 --> 00:26:34.180
and how individuals become spirit mediums

00:26:34.180 --> 00:26:37.505
in much fuller kind of details,

00:26:37.505 --> 00:26:40.040
taking apart hagiographies,

00:26:40.040 --> 00:26:43.400
taking apart oral accounts
of how individuals

00:26:43.400 --> 00:26:45.563
became spirit mediums.

00:26:46.610 --> 00:26:47.960
There's also a lot of documentation

00:26:47.960 --> 00:26:50.660
about how spirit mediums
sit at the center of,

00:26:50.660 --> 00:26:51.610
and are embedded

00:26:51.610 --> 00:26:56.560
within master-student
lineages of instruction

00:26:56.560 --> 00:26:58.740
of various sorts with actual individuals,

00:26:58.740 --> 00:26:59.910
and with mythic figures.

00:26:59.910 --> 00:27:02.320
And this was not really discussed much

00:27:02.320 --> 00:27:03.983
in the prior generations.

00:27:05.462 --> 00:27:07.280
There's a lot of work
on how spirit mediums

00:27:07.280 --> 00:27:10.600
sit at the center of, they
have an entourage of followers,

00:27:10.600 --> 00:27:13.870
a network of patrons, circles of clients.

00:27:13.870 --> 00:27:15.780
They have their own students as well.

00:27:15.780 --> 00:27:19.380
And so sort of describing
and mapping that is important

00:27:19.380 --> 00:27:21.950
for making sense of what
spirit possession is.

00:27:21.950 --> 00:27:24.502
And sometimes these groups

00:27:24.502 --> 00:27:27.970
even become more like
religious movements or cults

00:27:27.970 --> 00:27:31.683
of like even kind of broader scale.

00:27:32.750 --> 00:27:33.870
There's a lot of discussion

00:27:33.870 --> 00:27:38.010
about the way in which
these sort of spirit mediums

00:27:38.010 --> 00:27:41.903
found in cities are
following vocational careers.

00:27:43.150 --> 00:27:46.450
Prior studies of spirit
mediumship in rural areas

00:27:46.450 --> 00:27:47.283
would often talk

00:27:47.283 --> 00:27:50.610
about how individuals
who become spirit mediums

00:27:50.610 --> 00:27:52.860
would become possessed occasionally

00:27:52.860 --> 00:27:54.700
during certain kinds of ceremonies,

00:27:54.700 --> 00:27:57.746
or in certain kinds of crises,

00:27:57.746 --> 00:28:00.000
certain kinds of annual cycles,

00:28:00.000 --> 00:28:02.600
but the spirit mediums in these cities

00:28:02.600 --> 00:28:05.350
are basically like, they're
hanging up a shingle,

00:28:05.350 --> 00:28:08.444
and they are sort of
available to help people

00:28:08.444 --> 00:28:13.444
like every week and sometimes
multiple days during the week.

00:28:14.120 --> 00:28:18.720
So they really are sort of
like vocational careers.

00:28:18.720 --> 00:28:23.720
They are often religious
careers of virtuoso,

00:28:25.700 --> 00:28:28.110
in which that's actually a primary means

00:28:28.110 --> 00:28:29.810
by which they're supporting themselves

00:28:29.810 --> 00:28:31.240
in terms of livelihood.

00:28:31.240 --> 00:28:36.040
There's also a discussion about
how there's growing numbers.

00:28:36.040 --> 00:28:38.100
Everyone's talking about
how there's growing numbers

00:28:38.100 --> 00:28:41.960
of them across the country
and within regions,

00:28:41.960 --> 00:28:45.370
and the diversifying demographics of them.

00:28:45.370 --> 00:28:49.550
There used to be the
notion that spirit mediums

00:28:49.550 --> 00:28:52.977
were mostly women and/or
transgendered people,

00:28:54.810 --> 00:28:56.800
but increasingly there's recognition

00:28:56.800 --> 00:29:01.800
that there's cisgendered males
and other folks doing this.

00:29:03.024 --> 00:29:07.210
So that's part of the
sort of the making sense

00:29:07.210 --> 00:29:10.623
of who spirit mediums are and
what spirit possession is.

00:29:11.840 --> 00:29:13.480
And there's also this overwhelming sense

00:29:13.480 --> 00:29:17.530
that spirit mediums and
spirit possession itself

00:29:17.530 --> 00:29:19.830
is newly assertive as a public presence,

00:29:19.830 --> 00:29:23.750
and has a kind of prominence
in local settings,

00:29:23.750 --> 00:29:24.583
and in the country,

00:29:24.583 --> 00:29:25.640
both in terms of in person,

00:29:25.640 --> 00:29:28.730
the kind of rituals they're
performing in public,

00:29:28.730 --> 00:29:33.730
but also in terms of the mass media,

00:29:34.080 --> 00:29:36.450
Thai language mass media picks them up,

00:29:36.450 --> 00:29:38.803
looks at them, examines them,

00:29:39.890 --> 00:29:43.390
they write their own sort of
accounts of their experiences,

00:29:43.390 --> 00:29:46.173
and have those sort of
placed inside of mass media.

00:29:51.220 --> 00:29:52.630
The image of spirit mediums

00:29:52.630 --> 00:29:55.550
also focuses on how they forged

00:29:55.550 --> 00:29:58.110
social financial and ritual relations.

00:29:58.110 --> 00:30:00.530
Sometimes contentious,
sometimes idiosyncratic

00:30:00.530 --> 00:30:03.570
with Buddhist monks, typically
with them as junior partners.

00:30:03.570 --> 00:30:06.260
There's a lot more documentation

00:30:06.260 --> 00:30:11.210
about how spirit mediums have
these sort of somewhat tense,

00:30:11.210 --> 00:30:12.900
and complicated relationships
with particular monks

00:30:12.900 --> 00:30:15.997
in this or that location or temple

00:30:15.997 --> 00:30:20.997
engaged in joint kinds
of religious projects,

00:30:21.100 --> 00:30:22.520
or ritual activity,

00:30:22.520 --> 00:30:24.260
and that wasn't really emphasized,

00:30:24.260 --> 00:30:28.979
or even present in the prior
generation scholarship.

00:30:28.979 --> 00:30:30.760
And then there's just this general sense

00:30:30.760 --> 00:30:33.120
that spirit mediums

00:30:33.120 --> 00:30:36.040
are these innovative
religious entrepreneurs.

00:30:36.040 --> 00:30:39.760
There's a lot of documentation
about these new kinds of gods

00:30:39.760 --> 00:30:40.630
that are possessing them

00:30:40.630 --> 00:30:43.880
that are drawn from the Chinese pantheons,

00:30:43.880 --> 00:30:46.677
or Hindu pantheons, Indian pantheons,

00:30:47.910 --> 00:30:49.943
pantheons of royal figures,

00:30:51.670 --> 00:30:52.560
there's a lot of focus

00:30:52.560 --> 00:30:55.873
on they're doing new kinds
of rituals and techniques,

00:30:58.430 --> 00:31:00.910
some drawn from Buddhist actors,

00:31:00.910 --> 00:31:04.283
some drawn from astrologers, what-not.

00:31:05.420 --> 00:31:10.420
They talk a lot about
merit, Bon and barami,

00:31:10.590 --> 00:31:12.330
sort of perfected virtue,

00:31:12.330 --> 00:31:14.370
and other kinds of Buddhist terminologies

00:31:14.370 --> 00:31:16.307
make sense of their
experience of possession,

00:31:16.307 --> 00:31:19.070
and the power of the possessing deities.

00:31:19.070 --> 00:31:23.230
These deities are seen as
repositories of Bon barami.

00:31:23.230 --> 00:31:26.510
This is not the way people
talked about the kinds of spirits

00:31:26.510 --> 00:31:31.510
that possessed figures in the
prior generations studies.

00:31:32.790 --> 00:31:35.440
And there's a lot of discussion

00:31:35.440 --> 00:31:37.518
about how these sorts of spirit mediums

00:31:37.518 --> 00:31:41.690
display of behavior that
mimics normative ethos.

00:31:41.690 --> 00:31:44.010
They're like vegetarians,

00:31:44.010 --> 00:31:48.090
and they sometimes
display very prominently,

00:31:48.090 --> 00:31:53.090
they're celibate, that
they engage in meditation,

00:31:54.280 --> 00:31:57.780
that they sponsor merit-making activities,

00:31:57.780 --> 00:32:00.010
and all this other sorts of elements

00:32:00.010 --> 00:32:04.823
that make them look more
proper and normative and pious.

00:32:07.410 --> 00:32:09.880
These spirit mediums are
also shown as sort of...

00:32:11.750 --> 00:32:14.730
They're drawing on
mainstream Buddhist material

00:32:14.730 --> 00:32:15.890
and visual cultures.

00:32:15.890 --> 00:32:20.440
They make ambulates of the
gods that possess them,

00:32:20.440 --> 00:32:24.230
they make statues of
the gods, and whatnot.

00:32:24.230 --> 00:32:26.230
So they're sort of my more embedded

00:32:26.230 --> 00:32:31.230
in the kind of visual
material performative culture

00:32:33.220 --> 00:32:36.773
of Southeast Asian
Buddhism or Thai Buddhism.

00:32:38.630 --> 00:32:40.353
Very quickly, the analytic themes.

00:32:42.320 --> 00:32:47.320
There's this analysis of
how these spirit mediums

00:32:47.560 --> 00:32:50.710
are remaking village
religiosity in the city,

00:32:50.710 --> 00:32:54.270
either rural migrants who
have brought it with them,

00:32:54.270 --> 00:32:56.470
or mobile urbanites
who have encountered it

00:32:56.470 --> 00:32:59.700
in their travels around the country.

00:32:59.700 --> 00:33:00.970
There's analyses

00:33:00.970 --> 00:33:04.690
of how you basically have
an ethnic majority tie

00:33:04.690 --> 00:33:07.720
sort of appropriating
and reworking elements

00:33:07.720 --> 00:33:09.570
from ethnic minority religions,

00:33:09.570 --> 00:33:12.890
like Chinese popular religion,
or Indian popular religion

00:33:12.890 --> 00:33:16.440
in terms of their
religious self-identities,

00:33:16.440 --> 00:33:17.620
and how they're appropriating,

00:33:17.620 --> 00:33:20.293
and reworking royalist sacrality

00:33:21.230 --> 00:33:22.600
even as they're non-royals

00:33:22.600 --> 00:33:25.150
in the sense that they're possessed

00:33:25.150 --> 00:33:28.387
by these sort of past royal past kings

00:33:30.170 --> 00:33:32.113
and monarchs and royal figures.

00:33:35.710 --> 00:33:36.700
There's always this sense

00:33:36.700 --> 00:33:38.320
that these kinds of spirit mediums,

00:33:38.320 --> 00:33:42.450
this kind of possession is speaking to...

00:33:42.450 --> 00:33:46.210
It's a newly constituted
kind of religious identity,

00:33:46.210 --> 00:33:47.500
and vocational career,

00:33:47.500 --> 00:33:49.800
and that it's designed to satisfy

00:33:49.800 --> 00:33:50.777
new clienteles and followers, migrants,

00:33:50.777 --> 00:33:55.777
proletariats in the city,
professionalized middle classes,

00:33:59.350 --> 00:34:02.733
even educated globalized elites.

00:34:03.850 --> 00:34:06.700
And so there's this
sense of spirit mediums

00:34:06.700 --> 00:34:08.499
as these religious entrepreneurs

00:34:08.499 --> 00:34:12.284
who are trafficking and
providing often stigmatized

00:34:12.284 --> 00:34:15.820
form of worldly prosperity religion.

00:34:15.820 --> 00:34:19.060
People talk about them as
leaders of prosperity cults.

00:34:19.060 --> 00:34:22.260
Peter Jackson had put spirit mediums

00:34:22.260 --> 00:34:26.727
along with other kinds of
figures who led prosperity cults

00:34:26.727 --> 00:34:31.710
in the '80s and '90s and beyond
and he's written about that.

00:34:33.170 --> 00:34:36.990
And basically, when this second generation

00:34:36.990 --> 00:34:39.100
tries to conceptualize spirit possession

00:34:39.100 --> 00:34:40.960
in relation to Southeast Asian Buddhism,

00:34:40.960 --> 00:34:43.710
they talk about syncretism still,

00:34:43.710 --> 00:34:47.210
although there's more
tensions and anxiety about it,

00:34:47.210 --> 00:34:49.540
so I see it as sort of thinking about

00:34:49.540 --> 00:34:51.940
fractious inclusive syncretism.

00:34:51.940 --> 00:34:55.043
It doesn't seem to fit
as neatly as it used to,

00:34:55.043 --> 00:34:59.901
and some of them jettison
the idea of syncretism,

00:34:59.901 --> 00:35:02.070
and want to talk about
other things like hybridity,

00:35:02.070 --> 00:35:05.120
Pattana Kitiarsa's
Scholarship focuses on that.

00:35:05.120 --> 00:35:10.120
But the way spirit possession
is now seen as situated

00:35:10.240 --> 00:35:11.690
inside of Southeast Asian Buddhism

00:35:11.690 --> 00:35:14.760
is that it's sort of incorporating,

00:35:14.760 --> 00:35:17.950
and using normative Buddhist
beliefs and practices

00:35:17.950 --> 00:35:19.610
to justify their existence,

00:35:19.610 --> 00:35:22.630
a kind of Buddhacization of possession.

00:35:22.630 --> 00:35:25.230
And therefore there's more
discussion about idiosyncratic,

00:35:25.230 --> 00:35:28.020
or unorthodox and even heterodox ways

00:35:28.020 --> 00:35:29.320
that spirit mediums act

00:35:29.320 --> 00:35:32.160
with regards to Buddhist
beliefs and practices.

00:35:32.160 --> 00:35:34.950
Marjorie Muecke has this famous essay

00:35:34.950 --> 00:35:39.640
where she documents three monks

00:35:39.640 --> 00:35:41.940
who are essentially
students of a spirit medium

00:35:41.940 --> 00:35:46.940
and act, very clearly sort of
defer in terms of authority

00:35:48.580 --> 00:35:50.023
to the spirit medium.

00:35:51.230 --> 00:35:53.170
And there's more discussion

00:35:53.170 --> 00:35:55.240
about how there's this
tension and conflict

00:35:55.240 --> 00:35:57.310
between spirit mediums and Buddhist monks,

00:35:57.310 --> 00:36:00.710
as well as the Sangha and
the mainstream Buddhism,

00:36:00.710 --> 00:36:03.750
and spirit mediums are seen
as sort of speaking back,

00:36:03.750 --> 00:36:06.440
and criticizing the Sangha and monks,

00:36:06.440 --> 00:36:08.400
and sometimes mainstream Buddhists.

00:36:08.400 --> 00:36:10.520
So in all these ways,

00:36:10.520 --> 00:36:15.520
this second generation of
work on spirit possession

00:36:17.660 --> 00:36:20.460
in Buddhist Southeast Asia has emphasized

00:36:20.460 --> 00:36:21.980
the way it strains the image

00:36:22.825 --> 00:36:25.803
of this kind of uncontested
hegemonic inclusive syncretism,

00:36:27.330 --> 00:36:29.650
and that it strains the image

00:36:29.650 --> 00:36:31.460
that they're just a specialized niche

00:36:31.460 --> 00:36:35.250
providing unique but
relatively marginal services

00:36:35.250 --> 00:36:39.270
because they are providing
a wider set of services

00:36:39.270 --> 00:36:41.540
that are more conventional and mainstream.

00:36:41.540 --> 00:36:45.110
And that clearly this idea

00:36:45.110 --> 00:36:47.500
of a segregated, structural opposition

00:36:47.500 --> 00:36:52.402
between spirit mediums and
monks is seen as questionable,

00:36:52.402 --> 00:36:56.070
or sort of faltering.

00:36:56.070 --> 00:36:59.790
So then we come to the third
generation of scholarship,

00:36:59.790 --> 00:37:01.162
which is where I see

00:37:01.162 --> 00:37:05.670
the contributions to
this book being placed,

00:37:05.670 --> 00:37:08.939
and my trope I'm not totally
happy with it I'm interested.

00:37:08.939 --> 00:37:10.439
- [Woman] That's what we're hearing now.

00:37:10.439 --> 00:37:12.882
- [Woman] Oh, that's
what we're hearing, okay.

00:37:12.882 --> 00:37:15.840
- It is multiple spirit
possessions as alternative pieties

00:37:15.840 --> 00:37:18.284
within Buddhist Southeast Asia.

00:37:18.284 --> 00:37:22.222
So that's basically where I would place

00:37:22.222 --> 00:37:26.180
the works in this volume.

00:37:26.180 --> 00:37:28.603
They I think foreground.

00:37:34.620 --> 00:37:37.020
- Everybody bear with us
as we try to figure out

00:37:37.020 --> 00:37:39.623
what happened to Eric's connection.

00:37:44.630 --> 00:37:46.880
I'll send him a brief
email, just one second.

00:37:53.370 --> 00:37:56.620
- [Erick] And there's also this sense

00:37:56.620 --> 00:38:00.717
that they're engaged with
more than just monks.

00:38:00.717 --> 00:38:02.370
Usually the discussion was
always about spirit mediums,

00:38:02.370 --> 00:38:04.520
and monks relation to each other,

00:38:04.520 --> 00:38:08.120
but they're also seen
interacting with state brokers,

00:38:08.120 --> 00:38:10.093
and civil society, churches.

00:38:15.854 --> 00:38:18.603
They are now also are
even more deeply engaged

00:38:20.290 --> 00:38:22.160
with, like, culture industries,

00:38:22.160 --> 00:38:25.580
like footprint and digital
mass media and social media.

00:38:25.580 --> 00:38:30.580
They use social media to fan,
and preserve their entourages

00:38:32.110 --> 00:38:33.683
and to reach out to clientele,

00:38:34.935 --> 00:38:39.580
for families, the rituals
they take part in,

00:38:41.071 --> 00:38:43.203
and that actually sometimes

00:38:43.203 --> 00:38:46.313
there's even another element that's new

00:38:46.313 --> 00:38:50.173
is their social organization
becomes even more complex.

00:38:50.173 --> 00:38:51.191
A lot of their articles

00:38:51.191 --> 00:38:53.593
talk about how there was temporarily

00:38:53.593 --> 00:38:55.893
that kind of effort to
create an (indistinct).

00:39:00.350 --> 00:39:04.400
With formal kinds of positions
and authority and religions

00:39:04.400 --> 00:39:07.430
that it's apart for various readings

00:39:07.430 --> 00:39:09.803
that you can read the article about.

00:39:10.942 --> 00:39:12.480
And there's even cases of...

00:39:12.480 --> 00:39:14.080
Another thing that scholars have noticed,

00:39:14.080 --> 00:39:15.765
there's cases of sort of
role of spirit mediums

00:39:15.765 --> 00:39:19.920
and Buddhist monks actually
collapsing into each other.

00:39:19.920 --> 00:39:22.740
There's more documentation of individuals

00:39:22.740 --> 00:39:26.400
who ordained temporarily
as monks, but who are,

00:39:26.400 --> 00:39:28.930
and have been spirit mediums in the past

00:39:28.930 --> 00:39:31.125
and the complicated way they negotiate

00:39:31.125 --> 00:39:35.190
that kind of wearing both of those hats,

00:39:35.190 --> 00:39:37.253
even if only temporarily.

00:39:38.560 --> 00:39:41.760
And a final element that you
see a lot of the scholarship

00:39:41.760 --> 00:39:46.130
is that there's a sense that
(indistinct) and possession

00:39:46.992 --> 00:39:47.825
and position like behavior

00:39:47.825 --> 00:39:51.500
actually extends beyond just self-identity

00:39:51.500 --> 00:39:53.100
identified with spirit mediums,

00:39:53.100 --> 00:39:56.720
but that you find other
actors including monks,

00:39:56.720 --> 00:40:01.720
whether it's those visions or
dreams or translate behavior,

00:40:02.450 --> 00:40:04.580
they're doing things that echo

00:40:05.890 --> 00:40:08.170
what sort of looks like possession,

00:40:08.170 --> 00:40:09.530
and has possession like qualities.

00:40:09.530 --> 00:40:10.580
And they valorize it.

00:40:10.580 --> 00:40:13.040
They give it different
terms and languages,

00:40:13.040 --> 00:40:16.330
but there's a kind of similarity.

00:40:18.520 --> 00:40:21.947
And so basically analytically,

00:40:25.200 --> 00:40:28.040
the other thing that these
new scholarship looks at

00:40:28.040 --> 00:40:29.410
that you find in this book

00:40:29.410 --> 00:40:32.777
is that scholars are
situating spirit mediums

00:40:32.777 --> 00:40:34.440
and spirit possession in relation

00:40:34.440 --> 00:40:38.160
to vernacular Buddhist beliefs
and practices on the ground,

00:40:38.160 --> 00:40:41.751
rather than the official
literary Buddhisms

00:40:41.751 --> 00:40:45.360
that are conveying canonical
establishment, or elite voices.

00:40:45.360 --> 00:40:48.640
So there's sort of more round and room

00:40:48.640 --> 00:40:52.630
for (indistinct)
possession to sort of claim

00:40:52.630 --> 00:40:56.233
a kind of value and legitimate based on.

00:40:59.500 --> 00:41:01.870
And the other thing (indistinct) analytics

00:41:04.135 --> 00:41:08.270
is that again are...

00:41:09.610 --> 00:41:12.413
In the past, it was only
thinking about things

00:41:12.413 --> 00:41:15.330
in relation to like monks and
the Sangha, and/or the state,

00:41:15.330 --> 00:41:18.390
but a lot of attention is
paid to the way they relate

00:41:18.390 --> 00:41:21.883
to market in terms of
commercialism and commodities.

00:41:22.990 --> 00:41:24.780
The way they relate to civil society

00:41:24.780 --> 00:41:27.653
in terms of culture industries, media,

00:41:28.530 --> 00:41:32.773
academics, academic
scholarships, associations,

00:41:35.380 --> 00:41:38.112
cultural heritage, and tourism.

00:41:38.112 --> 00:41:39.810
So the broadening of the sense

00:41:39.810 --> 00:41:43.427
of how spirit possession
and spirit medium's side

00:41:44.527 --> 00:41:48.813
of the larger social terrain beyond that.

00:41:52.530 --> 00:41:55.430
So the final point I'm
gonna make, essentially,

00:41:55.430 --> 00:41:58.293
when it comes to conceptualizing
spirit possession,

00:41:58.293 --> 00:42:02.443
and Southeast Asian
Buddhism in this generation,

00:42:02.443 --> 00:42:04.023
or this third generation,

00:42:04.910 --> 00:42:06.866
people don't talk about syncretism,

00:42:06.866 --> 00:42:09.360
but there's a lot of anxiety about it,

00:42:09.360 --> 00:42:11.163
and how to characterize it.

00:42:12.350 --> 00:42:15.140
I've added adjectives when I
read people's scholarships,

00:42:15.140 --> 00:42:17.010
sometimes they seem to be talking about,

00:42:17.010 --> 00:42:18.880
like, siloed forms of syncretism,

00:42:18.880 --> 00:42:21.134
or Fisher forms of syncretism,

00:42:21.134 --> 00:42:24.716
or competitive forms of syncretism.

00:42:24.716 --> 00:42:26.450
Others have started to
try to reach for language

00:42:26.450 --> 00:42:29.280
like polytrophy or creolization

00:42:29.280 --> 00:42:31.514
as they continue to struggle to make sense

00:42:31.514 --> 00:42:33.880
of how spirit possession fits

00:42:33.880 --> 00:42:38.563
in the side of a Buddhist
dominated kind religious world.

00:42:39.750 --> 00:42:44.750
And so actually, there's
been this growing sense

00:42:47.110 --> 00:42:49.210
that like the conventional way

00:42:50.316 --> 00:42:54.050
of dividing up the religious
world that's mixed together

00:42:54.050 --> 00:42:55.360
is too limited, right?

00:42:55.360 --> 00:42:56.637
You can't just think about

00:42:56.637 --> 00:42:58.710
Buddhism and Brahmanism, and (indistinct)

00:42:58.710 --> 00:43:02.240
there's other elements,
whether (indistinct)

00:43:07.692 --> 00:43:10.320
number of other kinds of ways
of thinking about influences,

00:43:10.320 --> 00:43:12.150
and religious traditions

00:43:12.150 --> 00:43:16.210
that sort of make up the
total mix and push forward.

00:43:16.210 --> 00:43:19.300
That also opens up ways in
which where they locate.

00:43:19.300 --> 00:43:20.960
In the first series for the scholars,

00:43:20.960 --> 00:43:24.680
all located spirit possession
inside of sort of animism,

00:43:24.680 --> 00:43:27.940
which was the most subordinated element,

00:43:27.940 --> 00:43:29.277
but now you can see how spirit mediums

00:43:29.277 --> 00:43:30.970
and spirit possession can set

00:43:30.970 --> 00:43:32.760
inside of a lot of other kinds

00:43:32.760 --> 00:43:37.760
of visions of religious
tracing and practice

00:43:38.300 --> 00:43:40.456
that would expand the horizons

00:43:40.456 --> 00:43:45.440
of what this composite
Southeast Asian Buddhist world

00:43:45.440 --> 00:43:47.067
looks like historically.

00:43:49.000 --> 00:43:52.690
So the final point that I would make

00:43:52.690 --> 00:43:54.450
is that all of this is to say

00:43:54.450 --> 00:43:56.660
that amongst these most current scholars

00:43:56.660 --> 00:43:59.310
of spirit possession that
you will find in this book,

00:44:02.930 --> 00:44:04.550
for them it's much more difficult

00:44:04.550 --> 00:44:07.371
to claim that spirit
possession and spirit mediums

00:44:07.371 --> 00:44:12.371
are so easily or effectively
dominated, constrained,

00:44:12.500 --> 00:44:14.869
subordinated, peripheralized,

00:44:14.869 --> 00:44:18.670
for the marginal overarching
Buddhist social order,

00:44:18.670 --> 00:44:20.550
because they have been encountering

00:44:20.550 --> 00:44:22.740
more and more spirit mediums

00:44:22.740 --> 00:44:26.280
who themselves are
pushing back against that

00:44:26.280 --> 00:44:30.700
and claiming that if ever you
get inside, that you belong

00:44:30.700 --> 00:44:35.160
inside that world as
good, proper Buddhists.

00:44:35.160 --> 00:44:37.577
(indistinct)

00:44:40.947 --> 00:44:45.629
And so a lot of the spirit
mediums that I've encountered,

00:44:45.629 --> 00:44:49.296
and then the scholars
and self-studying that

00:44:50.380 --> 00:44:52.940
end up taking positions quite different

00:44:54.170 --> 00:44:56.200
from the first generation of scholar.

00:45:00.910 --> 00:45:04.068
The parameters of how to
(indistinct) spirit possession

00:45:04.068 --> 00:45:05.568
in Southeast Asia.

