00:45:47.740 --> 00:45:48.573
- I'm back.

00:45:49.770 --> 00:45:50.670
- Oh, you're back.

00:45:53.100 --> 00:45:57.280
- Okay. I'm gonna kill the phone.

00:45:57.280 --> 00:45:59.283
I'm getting a rebound from it.

00:46:00.270 --> 00:46:01.690
- Okay. Erik what-

00:46:01.690 --> 00:46:04.310
- So Thomas Bocha and then
Janet Hoskins, after that,

00:46:04.310 --> 00:46:05.210
go ahead.

00:46:05.210 --> 00:46:06.650
- Erick, can you hear me?

00:46:06.650 --> 00:46:08.050
- Yeah, I can hear you good.

00:46:08.970 --> 00:46:10.130
- Great. Thank you.

00:46:10.130 --> 00:46:15.000
Thanks for the talk,
super helpful, actually,

00:46:15.000 --> 00:46:18.540
to think about the kind
of the scope of the study.

00:46:18.540 --> 00:46:22.023
I have two questions.

00:46:23.100 --> 00:46:28.100
One is thinking about the second
generation of scholarship,

00:46:28.369 --> 00:46:30.453
as you described it,

00:46:31.310 --> 00:46:33.980
all sorts of language of innovation anew

00:46:33.980 --> 00:46:37.550
and transformation anew, anew, anew.

00:46:37.550 --> 00:46:41.190
And I'm wondering to what degree

00:46:43.190 --> 00:46:45.540
there's something really new happening?

00:46:45.540 --> 00:46:48.210
And to what degree do we
actually just see people

00:46:48.210 --> 00:46:51.030
paying attention to what's
been going on for a long time

00:46:51.030 --> 00:46:53.620
and the first generation of the...

00:46:53.620 --> 00:46:57.690
The pre 1965 and the first
generation of scholars

00:46:57.690 --> 00:47:00.680
just ignored it or didn't
realize it was going on,

00:47:00.680 --> 00:47:02.520
or what have you.

00:47:02.520 --> 00:47:03.480
The second question.

00:47:03.480 --> 00:47:06.240
And it really points to...

00:47:06.240 --> 00:47:08.816
It's really about the third generation

00:47:08.816 --> 00:47:13.690
or the contemporary spirit mediums

00:47:14.660 --> 00:47:17.237
in Mainland Southeast Asia,

00:47:17.237 --> 00:47:22.237
Nathan Kaplan's book on
"Monastic Education in Korea"

00:47:23.710 --> 00:47:25.940
points to really interesting ways

00:47:25.940 --> 00:47:30.940
in which Korean Buddhists in
their institutional structures

00:47:31.270 --> 00:47:36.270
are clearly reading European
and American scholarship

00:47:36.300 --> 00:47:40.490
and histories of Buddhist studies.

00:47:40.490 --> 00:47:44.380
And it informs Buddhist education in Korea

00:47:44.380 --> 00:47:46.653
in very interesting ways.

00:47:48.060 --> 00:47:50.720
And so there's this feedback loop.

00:47:50.720 --> 00:47:55.720
And I'm wondering to what
degree do we see spirit mediums

00:47:55.880 --> 00:48:00.163
in contemporary Thailand, for example,

00:48:01.120 --> 00:48:03.480
how much are they aware of the scholarship

00:48:03.480 --> 00:48:05.463
that you've just been talking about?

00:48:06.430 --> 00:48:07.340
Do they know about it

00:48:07.340 --> 00:48:11.850
or is it something that's
not visible to them.

00:48:11.850 --> 00:48:12.910
Thanks very much.

00:48:12.910 --> 00:48:14.120
- Okay. Great questions.

00:48:14.120 --> 00:48:16.530
So the first one, I think it is...

00:48:16.530 --> 00:48:20.130
Part of the distinctiveness

00:48:20.130 --> 00:48:21.750
between the first and
the second generation

00:48:21.750 --> 00:48:24.380
is they're different
generations of anthropologists

00:48:24.380 --> 00:48:27.390
so they're bringing different
assumptions and techniques,

00:48:27.390 --> 00:48:30.810
you see a lot more attention
to like cultural politics

00:48:30.810 --> 00:48:35.810
and historical dynamics
and historical ethnography

00:48:35.810 --> 00:48:38.040
in the second generation that
wasn't there in the first.

00:48:38.040 --> 00:48:39.900
And there is also this sense of...

00:48:43.040 --> 00:48:46.263
A lot of these second
generation anthropologists,

00:48:47.660 --> 00:48:52.660
they've migrated from
studying rural peasant society

00:48:53.020 --> 00:48:55.820
to other kinds of social domains.

00:48:55.820 --> 00:49:00.820
So part of the newness is that
they're looking anew, right?

00:49:02.576 --> 00:49:04.390
I have not been able to find

00:49:04.390 --> 00:49:07.611
any sustained ethnographic
studies of religion

00:49:07.611 --> 00:49:09.690
in Chiang Mai or Bangkok,

00:49:09.690 --> 00:49:13.600
that really sort of attend in much...

00:49:13.600 --> 00:49:16.140
That are actually set in
that earlier generation

00:49:16.140 --> 00:49:18.080
from '65 to '85,

00:49:18.080 --> 00:49:20.610
there aren't that many anthropologists

00:49:20.610 --> 00:49:22.923
doing work in urban areas, texture.

00:49:24.009 --> 00:49:24.842
Anthropologists doing work
in urban areas, texture.

00:49:24.842 --> 00:49:26.328
I mean, there's a bit here and there,

00:49:26.328 --> 00:49:28.080
and you can see little suggestive echos

00:49:28.080 --> 00:49:31.130
of what the second generation discovers.

00:49:31.130 --> 00:49:32.500
So I am quite certain

00:49:32.500 --> 00:49:36.240
that it was there to a certain
degree previously, right?

00:49:36.240 --> 00:49:37.470
And one way I do know that

00:49:37.470 --> 00:49:42.300
is there were some Japanese
skull anthropologists

00:49:42.300 --> 00:49:43.590
who did their traditionally,

00:49:43.590 --> 00:49:47.206
amazingly, like empirically
detailed studies

00:49:47.206 --> 00:49:49.760
of religion in Bangkok.

00:49:49.760 --> 00:49:54.030
And I finally got their
work translated into English

00:49:54.030 --> 00:49:55.661
and I could tell from the visuals,

00:49:55.661 --> 00:49:57.250
and they were doing work...

00:49:57.250 --> 00:49:59.174
The work I have is from the 70s

00:49:59.174 --> 00:50:01.480
and they're clearly discussing

00:50:02.960 --> 00:50:07.060
religious and social forms
organized around possession

00:50:07.060 --> 00:50:09.770
that are echoes of what
the scholars are finding

00:50:09.770 --> 00:50:14.430
from like the 80s on, they
don't look exactly the same.

00:50:14.430 --> 00:50:15.350
They're precursors

00:50:15.350 --> 00:50:17.250
that have their own kind of like dynamics.

00:50:17.250 --> 00:50:20.040
They seem to be coming
out of urban Chinese

00:50:20.040 --> 00:50:23.390
popular religiosity in Bangkok as much.

00:50:23.390 --> 00:50:27.647
So that suggests that in that case,

00:50:27.647 --> 00:50:31.860
it wasn't because the
ideas were transmitted

00:50:31.860 --> 00:50:34.530
from like the rural society and reworked,

00:50:34.530 --> 00:50:37.860
it's coming from an
different kind of odd talk

00:50:37.860 --> 00:50:39.110
and indigenous source

00:50:40.050 --> 00:50:42.670
that the prior generations
of anthropologists

00:50:42.670 --> 00:50:43.520
just tended not to see

00:50:43.520 --> 00:50:45.500
because they weren't
doing work in urban areas.

00:50:45.500 --> 00:50:47.720
So there's something deaf in only there,

00:50:47.720 --> 00:50:51.290
but the documentary evidence
is pretty fragmented

00:50:51.290 --> 00:50:53.360
and not very extensive,

00:50:53.360 --> 00:50:56.820
but I assume what you're
suggesting is true,

00:50:56.820 --> 00:50:59.860
and similarly, we actually sort of get

00:50:59.860 --> 00:51:01.800
a decreasing sense of these questions

00:51:01.800 --> 00:51:04.750
in terms of rural society
in the 80s and the 90s

00:51:04.750 --> 00:51:06.460
among Western scholar anthropologists,

00:51:06.460 --> 00:51:08.730
because they're doing the same kinds

00:51:08.730 --> 00:51:09.870
of intense studies there.

00:51:09.870 --> 00:51:13.323
So we can't even track how it's
changing in the rural areas.

00:51:13.323 --> 00:51:14.590
In the third generation,

00:51:14.590 --> 00:51:17.390
you have more scholars like
Benjamin Bowman and others

00:51:17.390 --> 00:51:20.520
looking at possession in rural areas,

00:51:20.520 --> 00:51:23.980
in the wake of all these
transformations and reimaginings,

00:51:23.980 --> 00:51:27.380
and seeing things
colliding in ways, right?

00:51:27.380 --> 00:51:31.030
And in terms of that kind
of whether mediums...

00:51:31.030 --> 00:51:35.600
So most of the spirit
mediums I've ever encountered

00:51:35.600 --> 00:51:37.740
in my work from the 90s on in Bangkok,

00:51:37.740 --> 00:51:39.640
that's where I did my research.

00:51:39.640 --> 00:51:44.640
They're not particularly
fluent in English.

00:51:45.270 --> 00:51:48.960
So the degree that they get
access to academic models,

00:51:48.960 --> 00:51:53.810
it's because they're reading
Thai language popular press,

00:51:53.810 --> 00:51:58.810
which often will draw
on Thai anthropologists

00:51:59.790 --> 00:52:02.640
who are studying religion
and contemporary society,

00:52:02.640 --> 00:52:04.080
and maybe even possession.

00:52:04.080 --> 00:52:05.841
And those Thai scholars

00:52:05.841 --> 00:52:09.630
have been informed by those seminal works

00:52:09.630 --> 00:52:13.410
by the first generation of
foreign scholars, right?

00:52:13.410 --> 00:52:15.960
Or even earlier ones like Thai scholars,

00:52:15.960 --> 00:52:18.055
like Pra Rajavaramuni
and whatnot like that.

00:52:18.055 --> 00:52:21.620
So they're getting it
maybe two or three degrees

00:52:21.620 --> 00:52:24.740
of distance and reserve
and mediation, right?

00:52:24.740 --> 00:52:26.060
And they're encountering it....

00:52:26.060 --> 00:52:28.140
Because they're encountering
in the mass media,

00:52:28.140 --> 00:52:33.140
There's much more are
spectacularization and drama

00:52:33.870 --> 00:52:37.520
around these kinds of
visions of what it means

00:52:37.520 --> 00:52:38.950
to be a spirit medium.

00:52:38.950 --> 00:52:43.950
There's not this sort of more
sort of reserve scholarly

00:52:44.490 --> 00:52:48.550
kind of annotation and
detail and notation.

00:52:48.550 --> 00:52:50.370
So I think some of it is seeping through.

00:52:50.370 --> 00:52:52.370
I mean, they're clearly reading...

00:52:52.370 --> 00:52:53.940
Spirit mediums I've always encountered,

00:52:53.940 --> 00:52:57.840
I mean, they're deeply reading,
like popular mass media

00:52:57.840 --> 00:52:59.010
and all this other stuff.

00:52:59.010 --> 00:53:01.275
And in fact now,

00:53:01.275 --> 00:53:04.440
I didn't talk about the
Thai language scholarship

00:53:04.440 --> 00:53:05.437
on spirit mediums,

00:53:05.437 --> 00:53:10.120
but I've come across recent
dissertations by Thai monks

00:53:10.120 --> 00:53:12.210
who have studied spirit mediums, right?

00:53:12.210 --> 00:53:15.172
So there's a complex kind
of conversation going on

00:53:15.172 --> 00:53:20.172
between academics and
spirit mediums as well,

00:53:20.200 --> 00:53:23.510
being mediated through language and class

00:53:23.510 --> 00:53:24.560
and all this other stuff.

00:53:24.560 --> 00:53:27.833
And there's a lot of Thai masters students

00:53:27.833 --> 00:53:32.833
who have gone out and done
studies of ceremonial cycles

00:53:32.930 --> 00:53:35.980
that involve spirit mediums and possession

00:53:35.980 --> 00:53:37.440
all over the country

00:53:37.440 --> 00:53:41.300
as parts of kind of local
ethnic or territorial identity

00:53:41.300 --> 00:53:42.690
and social worlds.

00:53:42.690 --> 00:53:47.240
And that's out there and it's
all available for download

00:53:47.240 --> 00:53:49.037
digital download now, right?

00:53:49.037 --> 00:53:51.610
And so it'd be hard to track,

00:53:51.610 --> 00:53:52.730
but I wouldn't be surprised

00:53:52.730 --> 00:53:54.960
if this stuff doesn't start reverberating

00:53:54.960 --> 00:53:57.550
to the greater degree
that they become literate

00:54:00.030 --> 00:54:01.960
in accessing and using

00:54:01.960 --> 00:54:06.540
and knowing about this
kind of Scholastic work

00:54:06.540 --> 00:54:08.800
about popular religion
and spirit possession,

00:54:08.800 --> 00:54:11.030
and mediumship that's
floating around out there.

00:54:11.030 --> 00:54:12.730
So thanks for the questions. Yeah.

00:54:13.660 --> 00:54:15.260
- Janet, we have a question from you

00:54:15.260 --> 00:54:17.373
and then after Janet from Karen Myers.

00:54:18.580 --> 00:54:19.557
- Okay. Thank you.

00:54:19.557 --> 00:54:22.640
I'd actually like to ask the
same question that you asked me

00:54:22.640 --> 00:54:25.023
during discussion Erick.

00:54:27.210 --> 00:54:29.590
Why do you think that
the Vietnamese studies

00:54:29.590 --> 00:54:32.674
of spirit possession have been siloed.

00:54:32.674 --> 00:54:35.580
After I provided one approximate answer,

00:54:35.580 --> 00:54:37.220
another one sort of occurred to me,

00:54:37.220 --> 00:54:39.760
but I'd rather hear directly
from you what you think.

00:54:39.760 --> 00:54:42.140
- Yeah, well, I'll offer up my thoughts

00:54:42.140 --> 00:54:44.000
and then I'd be interested
to hear what you think too.

00:54:44.000 --> 00:54:46.370
I mean, in general, I
have to be on honest,

00:54:46.370 --> 00:54:51.003
the study of religion
in Myanmar, Thailand,

00:54:53.620 --> 00:54:55.378
Laos and Cambodia

00:54:55.378 --> 00:55:00.378
has tended to not pay attention
to the Vietnamese world.

00:55:01.040 --> 00:55:03.550
I mean, across the board.

00:55:03.550 --> 00:55:06.010
I think part of it is not seen

00:55:06.010 --> 00:55:08.916
as part of this sort of
Theravada majority world.

00:55:08.916 --> 00:55:11.400
There are parts of Vietnam that
sort of have that presence.

00:55:11.400 --> 00:55:15.623
So it just, hasn't been part
of the training of scholars.

00:55:16.780 --> 00:55:18.090
It's more complicated

00:55:18.090 --> 00:55:21.560
because clearly there's a
kind of cynic world, right?

00:55:21.560 --> 00:55:24.170
A cynic kind of logic
of organizing religion

00:55:24.170 --> 00:55:26.573
and popular religion
that's going on there.

00:55:28.290 --> 00:55:31.010
And so it's a little more
challenging for scholars

00:55:31.010 --> 00:55:32.220
of Mainland Southeast Asia

00:55:32.220 --> 00:55:33.670
and Theravada Buddhist majority countries

00:55:33.670 --> 00:55:35.670
who have not been exposed

00:55:35.670 --> 00:55:38.290
to that kind of scholarship
and ideas and literature,

00:55:38.290 --> 00:55:40.240
but it is a problem
and it's kind of ironic

00:55:40.240 --> 00:55:42.882
because, it is very common

00:55:42.882 --> 00:55:46.220
in the history of Eastern Thai Buddhism

00:55:46.220 --> 00:55:48.170
to point to these important figures

00:55:48.170 --> 00:55:50.290
who have introduced innovation.

00:55:50.290 --> 00:55:54.500
And often it's these it's the Sino ties

00:55:54.500 --> 00:55:56.690
who have been sort of negotiating

00:55:56.690 --> 00:55:57.900
different religious worlds,

00:55:57.900 --> 00:56:01.430
and they have spun out new
ideas inside of Thai Buddhism

00:56:01.430 --> 00:56:03.550
and Thai religiosity in general.

00:56:03.550 --> 00:56:06.546
So the same logic would
apply to spirit possession

00:56:06.546 --> 00:56:09.860
because actually there's
a lot of Thai Buddhists,

00:56:09.860 --> 00:56:11.800
even if they're not Sino Thai

00:56:11.800 --> 00:56:12.900
who have now...

00:56:12.900 --> 00:56:17.064
They're exposed to the
Chinese Pantheon and logics,

00:56:17.064 --> 00:56:20.448
and they're claiming that
they're being possessed

00:56:20.448 --> 00:56:23.080
by Chinese gods of various
sorts and all of this,

00:56:23.080 --> 00:56:24.350
even if they're not Sino Thai

00:56:24.350 --> 00:56:27.030
in terms of their own
ethnic origins in history.

00:56:27.030 --> 00:56:32.030
And so it would make
sense for Thai scholars

00:56:34.240 --> 00:56:36.710
or for scholars of spirit
possession in Thailand

00:56:36.710 --> 00:56:40.730
and in the other countries
of Mainland Southeast Asia

00:56:40.730 --> 00:56:43.730
to really pay attention
more, not only to Vietnam,

00:56:43.730 --> 00:56:47.670
but to the Sinotic kind
of world of religiosity

00:56:47.670 --> 00:56:52.020
and how it has influenced
historically in the past

00:56:52.020 --> 00:56:52.853
and in the present

00:56:52.853 --> 00:56:56.690
what's going on in where they're working.

00:56:56.690 --> 00:56:58.410
And it's actually kind of...

00:56:59.256 --> 00:57:01.300
I'll be honest.

00:57:01.300 --> 00:57:03.660
Like I have diligently collected

00:57:03.660 --> 00:57:04.840
as much as the scholarship

00:57:04.840 --> 00:57:08.510
on Vietnamese spirit possession
and mediums as I can find,

00:57:08.510 --> 00:57:10.250
but I haven't had the
time to dig through it

00:57:10.250 --> 00:57:12.710
because in the detail I'd like to.

00:57:12.710 --> 00:57:16.944
But it's embarrassing
because between 2005 and now,

00:57:16.944 --> 00:57:19.860
there's been an explosion of monographs

00:57:19.860 --> 00:57:22.630
and edited volumes that
actually just dwarfs

00:57:22.630 --> 00:57:24.900
what's going on in the Mainland Theravada

00:57:24.900 --> 00:57:27.549
part of Southeast Asia,

00:57:27.549 --> 00:57:32.010
that's been studying
this for decades longer.

00:57:32.010 --> 00:57:34.509
And yet as a publishing kind of project,

00:57:34.509 --> 00:57:38.070
it has not produced edited
volumes and monographs.

00:57:38.070 --> 00:57:40.314
In the same way, there
are very few monographs

00:57:40.314 --> 00:57:45.314
focused in a narrow sense,
on spirit possession

00:57:45.520 --> 00:57:50.210
and spirit mediumship in
the Theravada majority part

00:57:50.210 --> 00:57:52.751
of Mainland Southeast Asia, right?

00:57:52.751 --> 00:57:55.860
So I think it's mostly
about training and exposure

00:57:55.860 --> 00:57:58.870
and maybe prop language, right?

00:57:58.870 --> 00:58:02.690
Like to a certain degree,
it's the usual problem.

00:58:02.690 --> 00:58:04.370
Like you'd have to study Vietnamese

00:58:04.370 --> 00:58:07.190
and it would be good
to know Chinese as well

00:58:07.190 --> 00:58:09.310
to really be able to
make sense of this stuff.

00:58:09.310 --> 00:58:14.310
And it's difficult because
of training and constraints.

00:58:15.920 --> 00:58:17.970
Were there other thoughts you had that...

00:58:19.460 --> 00:58:21.310
- I actually had a different response,

00:58:21.310 --> 00:58:23.150
which was more about the influence

00:58:23.150 --> 00:58:25.011
of Vietnamese language scholars.

00:58:25.011 --> 00:58:29.730
Partly because it was very difficult

00:58:29.730 --> 00:58:32.390
to study religion at one point in Vietnam.

00:58:32.390 --> 00:58:35.080
And the fact that it
has become more possible

00:58:35.080 --> 00:58:37.210
for Vietnamese scholars
and for foreign scholars

00:58:37.210 --> 00:58:39.560
is something we owe in part

00:58:39.560 --> 00:58:43.270
to a particular folklore
institute run by Nodi Tin

00:58:43.270 --> 00:58:44.833
that has been very influential.

00:58:46.580 --> 00:58:48.680
But I think what's also true

00:58:48.680 --> 00:58:50.170
is that when you talk to people

00:58:50.170 --> 00:58:52.200
from other parts of Southeast Asia,

00:58:52.200 --> 00:58:54.540
they say, well, the Vietnamese
aren't really as Buddhist

00:58:54.540 --> 00:58:58.740
as we are, which may be a
kind of local ethnocentricism,

00:58:58.740 --> 00:59:00.340
they're not Theravada Buddhists.

00:59:01.604 --> 00:59:05.620
And certainly Buddhism doesn't
have the place in Vietnam

00:59:05.620 --> 00:59:07.030
that it has in Thailand.

00:59:07.030 --> 00:59:08.870
The place of tremendous power

00:59:08.870 --> 00:59:12.840
and sort of central religious influence,

00:59:12.840 --> 00:59:14.950
because Buddhism is competing

00:59:14.950 --> 00:59:19.042
against many other forms of religiosity.

00:59:19.042 --> 00:59:24.042
And one thing that this
folklore institute did

00:59:24.260 --> 00:59:26.190
is they labeled spirit possession

00:59:26.190 --> 00:59:27.383
as a (speaks in foreign language).

00:59:29.020 --> 00:59:31.040
And that in itself makes it in a sense,

00:59:31.040 --> 00:59:32.663
independent from Buddhism.

00:59:33.510 --> 00:59:35.660
Although there are a lot
of these collaborations

00:59:35.660 --> 00:59:38.510
that you're talking about,
and certainly there are monks

00:59:38.510 --> 00:59:41.170
who collaborate with spirit
mediums and so forth.

00:59:41.170 --> 00:59:45.080
But that has sort of
reconfigured the religious field

00:59:45.080 --> 00:59:46.230
in interesting ways.

00:59:46.230 --> 00:59:47.637
- Yeah, no, I think that's all true.

00:59:47.637 --> 00:59:51.210
And I don't think heritage
and folklore studies

00:59:51.210 --> 00:59:52.970
sort of has played the same sort of thing

00:59:52.970 --> 00:59:55.193
in the Theravada Mainland world.

00:59:56.535 --> 00:59:58.230
And there is something

00:59:58.230 --> 01:00:02.540
about the complicated sectarianism, right?

01:00:02.540 --> 01:00:04.440
Even in terms of Buddhism, but beyond that

01:00:04.440 --> 01:00:08.520
of the religious world of
Vietnam that is confusing

01:00:08.520 --> 01:00:10.740
and challenging to scholars

01:00:10.740 --> 01:00:13.570
trained to think about religious worlds

01:00:13.570 --> 01:00:14.980
that are clearly dominated

01:00:14.980 --> 01:00:18.550
by a kind of Theravadian sectarian,

01:00:18.550 --> 01:00:20.923
whatever it's local vernacularization,

01:00:20.923 --> 01:00:24.880
a singular Theravadian and
kind of orthodoxy, right?

01:00:24.880 --> 01:00:27.230
So I think that's part
of it too. Yeah, thanks.

01:00:30.770 --> 01:00:33.061
- Karen. You can go ahead.

01:00:33.061 --> 01:00:33.894
- Okay. Thanks.

01:00:33.894 --> 01:00:34.727
Thanks for that talk.

01:00:34.727 --> 01:00:37.482
I really appreciated
the literature review.

01:00:37.482 --> 01:00:40.833
I work more in Tibetan Buddhism,

01:00:41.920 --> 01:00:46.170
but so I was curious as to
how much of the literature,

01:00:47.080 --> 01:00:50.280
you talked a little bit
about cross-cultural analysis

01:00:50.280 --> 01:00:53.270
between these Southeast Asian countries,

01:00:53.270 --> 01:00:57.140
but how much of the
literature really engages?

01:00:57.140 --> 01:00:59.070
I mean, it's a worldwide phenomena, right?

01:00:59.070 --> 01:01:00.690
Spirit possession.

01:01:00.690 --> 01:01:03.540
And if you could cite any works

01:01:03.540 --> 01:01:07.150
that really engage in
that comparative question

01:01:07.150 --> 01:01:08.133
in a robust way?

01:01:09.230 --> 01:01:12.230
- Well, I have to say,
I've always been surprised

01:01:12.230 --> 01:01:14.589
that scholars working on spirit possession

01:01:14.589 --> 01:01:17.640
in Theravada Buddhist Southeast Asia

01:01:19.400 --> 01:01:22.630
have not tended to look outside the region

01:01:22.630 --> 01:01:25.970
and the sectarian orders
the way I would've imagined.

01:01:25.970 --> 01:01:28.190
Again, I have large
collections of scholarship

01:01:28.190 --> 01:01:32.350
on possession in Tibetan Buddhism
and even China and India.

01:01:32.350 --> 01:01:33.700
Especially Sri Lanka.

01:01:33.700 --> 01:01:36.280
I gotta say, that's the
most striking thing.

01:01:36.280 --> 01:01:38.400
Most scholars working
on spirit possession,

01:01:38.400 --> 01:01:41.244
In Myanmar or Thailand, Cambodia,

01:01:41.244 --> 01:01:42.700
they never think about the Sri Lanka case,

01:01:42.700 --> 01:01:44.383
which is just so obvious.

01:01:46.100 --> 01:01:47.440
John Holt has seen this book

01:01:47.440 --> 01:01:48.447
and he was kind of like,

01:01:48.447 --> 01:01:51.260
"Where's Sri Lanka in this project?"

01:01:51.260 --> 01:01:52.568
And I was like,

01:01:52.568 --> 01:01:53.610
well, they envisioned
it in a regional sense.

01:01:53.610 --> 01:01:55.630
So they didn't go there
but there's another reason.

01:01:55.630 --> 01:01:56.765
For whatever reason,

01:01:56.765 --> 01:02:00.650
it's just that linguistic
cultural zone and training thing.

01:02:00.650 --> 01:02:02.470
They just don't seem to look there.

01:02:02.470 --> 01:02:04.810
Again, just like in Vietnam,

01:02:04.810 --> 01:02:08.760
there's a large body of
monographs and volumes

01:02:08.760 --> 01:02:11.900
and other stuff about spirit
possession in Sri Lanka.

01:02:11.900 --> 01:02:15.380
And yet I don't find
scholars thinking about it.

01:02:15.380 --> 01:02:16.809
I try to think about it,

01:02:16.809 --> 01:02:20.089
but honestly, it's I think...

01:02:20.089 --> 01:02:21.785
I don't know.

01:02:21.785 --> 01:02:23.960
And then going beyond that,

01:02:23.960 --> 01:02:26.820
I mean, spirit possession looks different.

01:02:26.820 --> 01:02:30.160
Like in India it looks very
different in Tibet, right?

01:02:30.160 --> 01:02:34.460
And then I mean, like East
Asia is another world, right?

01:02:34.460 --> 01:02:36.320
You could have a interesting
comparative volume

01:02:36.320 --> 01:02:37.640
about how spirit possession

01:02:37.640 --> 01:02:39.790
is configured differently in these regions

01:02:39.790 --> 01:02:41.250
and the sectarian traditions.

01:02:41.250 --> 01:02:44.697
And I hope people try to sort of open up

01:02:47.890 --> 01:02:50.530
those kinds of conversations and projects,

01:02:50.530 --> 01:02:52.440
because I think it would be very fruitful

01:02:52.440 --> 01:02:56.210
and would energize the
study to see the kinds of...

01:02:56.210 --> 01:02:57.510
I mean, I've taken part

01:02:57.510 --> 01:02:59.974
in these sort of these
workshops on Buddhism

01:02:59.974 --> 01:03:02.454
that's had been run in Paris

01:03:02.454 --> 01:03:06.080
and that kind of cross-sectarian,
cross-regionalism

01:03:06.080 --> 01:03:09.760
has been very instructive
and informative for me

01:03:09.760 --> 01:03:14.393
thinking about what I'm seeing
on the ground in the present,

01:03:15.730 --> 01:03:17.910
but also what people have talked
about in terms of the past,

01:03:17.910 --> 01:03:21.180
in terms of Mainland South
East Asia and/or Thailand.

01:03:21.180 --> 01:03:26.180
So you contact me sort of
I'd have to stop and think

01:03:27.250 --> 01:03:30.480
about the scholarship
that actually does try

01:03:30.480 --> 01:03:33.900
to make those sorts of connections.

01:03:33.900 --> 01:03:35.274
Honestly, a lot of the anthropologists

01:03:35.274 --> 01:03:38.070
have been only intermittently
engaged with...

01:03:38.070 --> 01:03:41.500
There's a whole body of
concepts, analytical,

01:03:41.500 --> 01:03:43.430
theoretical work about
just spirit possession

01:03:43.430 --> 01:03:45.560
inside of anthropology.

01:03:45.560 --> 01:03:50.560
And it hasn't had a
very significant impact

01:03:51.075 --> 01:03:52.410
for the most part.

01:03:52.410 --> 01:03:53.243
It's interesting.

01:03:53.243 --> 01:03:58.060
I sometimes Thai study
scholars have drank the Koolaid

01:03:58.060 --> 01:04:00.430
of a kind of exceptionalism
a little too much,

01:04:00.430 --> 01:04:04.560
and it's sort of made them
inoculated against doing that.

01:04:04.560 --> 01:04:08.217
But so I'm not sure why,

01:04:08.217 --> 01:04:13.217
so there's a lot of other right
kinds of theoretical works

01:04:14.120 --> 01:04:16.170
either coming out of other regions.

01:04:16.170 --> 01:04:21.170
And I would say the introduction
by Peter and Benedicte

01:04:21.812 --> 01:04:24.860
does try to engage with
some of this stuff,

01:04:24.860 --> 01:04:26.900
think critically about how that absence

01:04:26.900 --> 01:04:30.160
has influenced past
scholarship and our approaches.

01:04:30.160 --> 01:04:33.750
They don't sort of rectify
the problem, but they note.

01:04:33.750 --> 01:04:35.395
I mean, they basically argue

01:04:35.395 --> 01:04:39.110
that there is a Southeast
Asian possession complex,

01:04:39.110 --> 01:04:40.560
and I agree and disagree with them

01:04:40.560 --> 01:04:41.930
in certain ways about that.

01:04:41.930 --> 01:04:43.160
But they're basically like

01:04:43.160 --> 01:04:45.930
let's think about regional possession

01:04:45.930 --> 01:04:48.150
in terms of like different kinds

01:04:48.150 --> 01:04:50.440
of regionalized kind of complexes

01:04:50.440 --> 01:04:52.520
that have a different distinct kind

01:04:52.520 --> 01:04:54.630
of historical and social logics.

01:04:54.630 --> 01:04:57.930
And they suggest that scholars

01:04:57.930 --> 01:05:01.290
working on Buddhi Southeast
Asia start thinking like that.

01:05:01.290 --> 01:05:04.460
So there are efforts to
sort of push that way,

01:05:04.460 --> 01:05:05.733
but it's not clear

01:05:05.733 --> 01:05:09.810
how much it'll be taken
up by future scholars.

01:05:09.810 --> 01:05:10.643
- Yeah. Thank you.

01:05:10.643 --> 01:05:12.230
It seems because one of the questions

01:05:12.230 --> 01:05:14.663
is how does it relate to Buddhism?

01:05:15.694 --> 01:05:17.050
And so we could look at
other Buddhist cultures,

01:05:17.050 --> 01:05:19.820
but also non Buddhist cultures
that have spirit possession

01:05:19.820 --> 01:05:22.593
to get a sort of broader perspective.

01:05:24.630 --> 01:05:26.530
- Actually, your comment makes me say,

01:05:28.240 --> 01:05:30.560
so the earliest scholars laid down

01:05:30.560 --> 01:05:34.210
these kinds of foundational
sort of frames and assumptions,

01:05:34.210 --> 01:05:37.410
and the fact that the
the earliest scholars,

01:05:37.410 --> 01:05:41.460
Spiro and Tambiah and Kirsch

01:05:41.460 --> 01:05:43.560
were working with this sort of issue

01:05:43.560 --> 01:05:47.570
of great traditions, little
traditions, and Buddhism

01:05:47.570 --> 01:05:49.180
and localization and all of that,

01:05:49.180 --> 01:05:51.930
that has tended to then sort of close out

01:05:51.930 --> 01:05:54.720
other kinds of optics and horizons

01:05:54.720 --> 01:05:58.482
and made scholars a little content.

01:05:58.482 --> 01:05:59.315
Later, generations of scholars

01:05:59.315 --> 01:06:02.760
just sort of not see analytic
and theoretical possibilities

01:06:02.760 --> 01:06:05.000
because they're like,
that's the big issue?

01:06:05.000 --> 01:06:07.020
How does spirit possession fit

01:06:07.020 --> 01:06:10.443
inside of Buddhist logics and actors

01:06:10.443 --> 01:06:12.270
and the Buddhist religious field.

01:06:12.270 --> 01:06:14.970
But what about all these
other kinds of questions?

01:06:14.970 --> 01:06:17.280
They tend to be just sort of...

01:06:17.280 --> 01:06:18.850
They aren't the are not on the horizon

01:06:18.850 --> 01:06:21.160
and so scholars don't
always sort of see them.

01:06:21.160 --> 01:06:22.560
They don't jump out at them.

01:06:23.861 --> 01:06:24.866
- Yeah. Thank you.

01:06:24.866 --> 01:06:25.699
And I did sort of frame it

01:06:25.699 --> 01:06:28.923
in terms of that particular
line of argument.

01:06:33.770 --> 01:06:34.820
- This is really great.

01:06:34.820 --> 01:06:37.480
Karen, I wanted to thank
you for that question

01:06:37.480 --> 01:06:40.394
because it's inspired me to
talk to some of my colleagues

01:06:40.394 --> 01:06:43.210
across the different area councils.

01:06:43.210 --> 01:06:45.470
Where Southeast Asian
studies with my colleagues

01:06:45.470 --> 01:06:48.690
in East Asian studies
and South Asian studies

01:06:48.690 --> 01:06:49.760
and other places too

01:06:49.760 --> 01:06:51.970
interested in these kinds of questions.

01:06:51.970 --> 01:06:52.803
And then I always think,

01:06:52.803 --> 01:06:54.500
well, we have some initiatives

01:06:54.500 --> 01:06:57.390
to think of a kind of
trans-regional Buddhist studies,

01:06:57.390 --> 01:07:01.298
but it also makes us maybe need
to rethink that too, right?

01:07:01.298 --> 01:07:04.620
There's certainly great reason to do that,

01:07:04.620 --> 01:07:05.530
but to think

01:07:05.530 --> 01:07:08.020
maybe the transnational
spirit possession studies

01:07:08.020 --> 01:07:10.210
or something would be a really interesting

01:07:10.210 --> 01:07:11.533
kind of framework as well.

01:07:12.910 --> 01:07:15.960
I said that Tak had a question,

01:07:15.960 --> 01:07:18.760
but in fact, he was waving to one of us.

01:07:18.760 --> 01:07:20.680
So I don't want force
him to have a question,

01:07:20.680 --> 01:07:23.740
but knowing Tak, he always
has a good question to ask.

01:07:23.740 --> 01:07:25.930
So I'll give you the opportunity, Tak,

01:07:25.930 --> 01:07:28.340
if you wanna take up the offer.

01:07:28.340 --> 01:07:30.733
- Actually, I was waiting at Rick Roof.

01:07:32.470 --> 01:07:36.631
Anyway, the, the question
I had was already answered

01:07:36.631 --> 01:07:39.110
because when listening to all this,

01:07:39.110 --> 01:07:44.110
I was thinking about spirit
procession in Southeast Asia.

01:07:44.780 --> 01:07:48.750
And the other place that a
lot of studies have been made

01:07:48.750 --> 01:07:50.203
is actually the Philippines.

01:07:51.650 --> 01:07:53.630
And so I was gonna ask Erick,

01:07:53.630 --> 01:07:57.104
whether instead of talking
about just Buddhism,

01:07:57.104 --> 01:07:59.990
if he could talk about spirit possession

01:07:59.990 --> 01:08:02.770
and the Philippines is an
interesting case, right?

01:08:02.770 --> 01:08:05.220
It's Catholic or non-Catholic.

01:08:05.220 --> 01:08:07.341
- It is, right for the broader...

01:08:07.341 --> 01:08:10.040
But again for, I think scholars

01:08:10.040 --> 01:08:13.120
working in mainland Southeast
Asia, they're intimidated.

01:08:13.120 --> 01:08:14.741
When you go to the insular world,

01:08:14.741 --> 01:08:19.141
it's not just social,
cultural, political logics,

01:08:19.141 --> 01:08:20.620
an organization would be different,

01:08:20.620 --> 01:08:24.710
but then you're encountering
Islam and Christianity, right?

01:08:24.710 --> 01:08:28.350
In a certain sense as these
world historical traditions.

01:08:28.350 --> 01:08:30.947
And there is a sense that
monotheistic traditions

01:08:30.947 --> 01:08:33.680
have treated spirit
possessions slightly different

01:08:33.680 --> 01:08:35.130
than non monotheist traditions,

01:08:35.130 --> 01:08:37.403
have been less accommodating often.

01:08:38.550 --> 01:08:41.310
At least, scholarships
tend to suggest that.

01:08:41.310 --> 01:08:42.930
So it's not like things
couldn't be learned,

01:08:42.930 --> 01:08:45.180
but I think again, they
see it as a little...

01:08:45.180 --> 01:08:49.240
I think it would be an easier step up

01:08:49.240 --> 01:08:52.640
to look at like possession
in the Chinese world

01:08:52.640 --> 01:08:55.770
and in the Vietnamese world
and in the south Asian world

01:08:55.770 --> 01:08:58.670
to the degree

01:08:58.670 --> 01:09:01.870
that they're in this
sort of non monotheistic

01:09:01.870 --> 01:09:04.280
dominated kind of religious orders.

01:09:04.280 --> 01:09:07.700
And it's another step up
then to take into account

01:09:07.700 --> 01:09:09.340
what's going on with spirit possession

01:09:09.340 --> 01:09:14.340
when you're in a Christian
or an Islamic kind of world

01:09:15.260 --> 01:09:18.400
that has often seems have
stronger prohibitions

01:09:18.400 --> 01:09:20.003
to exclude it, right?

01:09:22.988 --> 01:09:25.040
Despite early scholars
thinking that spirit possession

01:09:25.040 --> 01:09:27.608
is this subordinated
marginal sort of thing.

01:09:27.608 --> 01:09:30.220
It's recognized by Buddhists

01:09:30.220 --> 01:09:35.220
and it is accommodated and
tolerated in certain ways.

01:09:36.020 --> 01:09:38.650
Except for some of the most high modernist

01:09:39.550 --> 01:09:42.700
kinds of Thai or Buddhist
or Myanmar Buddhists

01:09:42.700 --> 01:09:44.233
who denounce it thoroughly.

01:09:45.270 --> 01:09:47.972
But there's a kind of
accepting accommodation of it,

01:09:47.972 --> 01:09:51.000
which can sometimes seem to be harder

01:09:51.000 --> 01:09:55.210
in certain kinds of Christian and Muslim

01:09:55.210 --> 01:09:56.540
kinds of environments.

01:09:56.540 --> 01:09:58.886
So it would definitely be...

01:09:58.886 --> 01:10:02.347
Especially if we wanna work
the regional kind of character.

01:10:02.347 --> 01:10:06.030
If Benedicte and Peter are talking

01:10:06.030 --> 01:10:10.330
about a Southeast Asian
sort of possession complex,

01:10:10.330 --> 01:10:14.010
they are mostly thinking about
it in terms of the Mainland,

01:10:14.010 --> 01:10:19.010
They haven't expanded that
horizon to the insular world,

01:10:19.706 --> 01:10:23.773
which would introduce certain
kinds of complexities.

01:10:28.890 --> 01:10:29.930
Thanks.
- I received a note

01:10:29.930 --> 01:10:33.503
from Rick Ruth who got the
spirit of the wave from Tak

01:10:34.358 --> 01:10:36.680
and now wishes to answer
a question as well.

01:10:36.680 --> 01:10:38.160
- I'm sorry. Yeah, yeah.

01:10:38.160 --> 01:10:39.650
Just actually two things really quick.

01:10:39.650 --> 01:10:41.900
First of all, I wanna
had to wish Erick White

01:10:41.900 --> 01:10:43.200
a happy birthday (laughs).

01:10:46.838 --> 01:10:48.250
I did remember it was your birthday

01:10:48.250 --> 01:10:49.923
and thank him for doing this.

01:10:51.000 --> 01:10:53.984
And actually I was
thinking a lot maybe about,

01:10:53.984 --> 01:10:57.223
in some ways we've been
talking about blending,

01:10:57.223 --> 01:11:00.160
what we've seen as Asian American studies

01:11:00.160 --> 01:11:01.740
with regional studies.

01:11:01.740 --> 01:11:03.890
A lot lately, I noticed
more and more departments

01:11:03.890 --> 01:11:07.540
are combining or considering 'em together.

01:11:07.540 --> 01:11:12.116
And this afternoon we've been
talking about in some ways,

01:11:12.116 --> 01:11:13.890
the geographical siloing, I guess,

01:11:13.890 --> 01:11:17.450
between Vietnam and Thailand
and the Philippines.

01:11:17.450 --> 01:11:19.780
Now, what I've felt myself wondering

01:11:19.780 --> 01:11:23.342
is in terms of diasporic studies,

01:11:23.342 --> 01:11:27.940
are there studies of spirit
medium even specifically

01:11:27.940 --> 01:11:32.890
Thai Kun Sojao turning
up in the United States.

01:11:32.890 --> 01:11:34.260
I mean, in our lifetime,

01:11:34.260 --> 01:11:36.630
in this kind of half century
that you've described,

01:11:36.630 --> 01:11:41.630
we've seen Thai world appearing
in more and more states

01:11:42.310 --> 01:11:47.150
across the country as
part of that manifestation

01:11:47.150 --> 01:11:50.320
of Southeast Asian religion
in North America or in Europe,

01:11:50.320 --> 01:11:53.730
are there Kun Sojao in the United States,

01:11:53.730 --> 01:11:56.060
are there anthropologists studying?

01:11:56.060 --> 01:11:58.543
- So interestingly,

01:11:59.452 --> 01:12:01.820
I'm not sure I know the literature

01:12:01.820 --> 01:12:04.730
as well as I could or should,

01:12:04.730 --> 01:12:07.583
and I think off the top of my head,

01:12:09.610 --> 01:12:12.880
it's in terms of the diaspora in America,

01:12:12.880 --> 01:12:15.090
and it's interestingly,

01:12:15.090 --> 01:12:17.800
I have seen analyses of spirit possession

01:12:17.800 --> 01:12:20.950
in sort of Southeast
Asian diasporic America

01:12:20.950 --> 01:12:24.170
in the Cambodian and the Laosian worlds

01:12:24.170 --> 01:12:26.215
because of the demographics

01:12:26.215 --> 01:12:30.670
of refugees and migration, right?

01:12:30.670 --> 01:12:32.480
I wouldn't be surprised
if it's not popping up

01:12:32.480 --> 01:12:35.183
in Burmese and Thai worlds as well,

01:12:36.210 --> 01:12:40.935
but I've seen a few pieces of
scholarship, but not a lot.

01:12:40.935 --> 01:12:43.910
So it is sort of...

01:12:44.810 --> 01:12:46.010
There is a touch of that,

01:12:46.010 --> 01:12:49.700
but I've never seen anything
about spirit possession

01:12:49.700 --> 01:12:54.213
amongst ethnic ties
outside of South East Asia

01:12:55.960 --> 01:12:58.080
or the extended sort of world

01:12:58.080 --> 01:13:00.795
on the borderlands, for instance.

01:13:00.795 --> 01:13:01.950
- So maybe this is somewhere

01:13:01.950 --> 01:13:04.930
where then a fourth generation
can go with it then, or-

01:13:04.930 --> 01:13:09.620
- Yeah, I mean, that's
certainly one direction.

01:13:09.620 --> 01:13:10.950
I mean, there's so many
different directions.

01:13:10.950 --> 01:13:13.660
I talk about issues and
directions in my afterword

01:13:13.660 --> 01:13:16.942
and Peter and Benedicte talk
about it as well in their book,

01:13:16.942 --> 01:13:20.810
I think actually, ironically,

01:13:20.810 --> 01:13:24.256
in relation to what Janet
was talking about or Karen,

01:13:24.256 --> 01:13:29.256
there's actually a sense
of going more intensely

01:13:29.520 --> 01:13:32.040
into the Southeast Asian world,

01:13:32.040 --> 01:13:33.810
which is to say,

01:13:33.810 --> 01:13:36.060
increasingly scholars are
pushing back on the idea

01:13:36.060 --> 01:13:40.120
of, okay, so there's this common
kind of Theravada Buddhist

01:13:41.670 --> 01:13:43.070
religious logic

01:13:43.070 --> 01:13:45.900
across all of mainland
Southeast Asia, right?

01:13:45.900 --> 01:13:48.890
But they're like, if you
look at the pantheons,

01:13:48.890 --> 01:13:51.620
the pantheons don't look the same,

01:13:51.620 --> 01:13:54.500
the pantheons of possessing
spirits don't look the same

01:13:54.500 --> 01:13:58.060
across the countries and
even regions within country.

01:13:58.060 --> 01:14:00.170
And you can see the language

01:14:00.170 --> 01:14:03.830
and even the kinds of the
styles and kind the modalities

01:14:03.830 --> 01:14:07.380
of spirit possession that are valorized

01:14:07.380 --> 01:14:10.430
and worth cultivating,

01:14:10.430 --> 01:14:13.810
they don't look the same
across these different worlds.

01:14:13.810 --> 01:14:15.740
So there's actually a increasingly a sense

01:14:15.740 --> 01:14:17.570
that we have to pay more attention

01:14:17.570 --> 01:14:20.490
to actually the historical
and social specificity

01:14:20.490 --> 01:14:25.490
of what these religious
worlds and fields look like

01:14:25.550 --> 01:14:30.550
underneath the umbrella
of the nation state,

01:14:30.580 --> 01:14:33.660
which tends to sort of
artificially homogenize everything

01:14:33.660 --> 01:14:36.320
much less the umbrella of Theravada

01:14:36.320 --> 01:14:39.710
which it can even more
powerfully homogenize everything.

01:14:39.710 --> 01:14:41.227
And there's a sense that actually,

01:14:41.227 --> 01:14:45.230
you've got very localized pantheons

01:14:45.230 --> 01:14:49.000
that are kind of open ended
and generatively playing.

01:14:49.000 --> 01:14:51.000
And Paul Sorrentino's article

01:14:52.245 --> 01:14:53.950
about out Vietnamese spirit possession

01:14:53.950 --> 01:14:56.850
in the edited volume
goes into great detail

01:14:56.850 --> 01:15:01.850
about sort of the creative
ambiguities and slippages

01:15:03.780 --> 01:15:05.500
within the Vietnamese Pantheon

01:15:05.500 --> 01:15:07.720
that has allowed spirit possession

01:15:07.720 --> 01:15:10.770
to take on these different
shapes over from the colonial era

01:15:10.770 --> 01:15:15.770
to the modern era and
actually jostling with visions

01:15:16.020 --> 01:15:18.553
of what the Pantheon
is and who's in and out

01:15:18.553 --> 01:15:21.240
and the logics of interacting with them.

01:15:21.240 --> 01:15:22.732
But the same thing, I think, applies

01:15:22.732 --> 01:15:26.440
to the local Theravada Buddhist worlds

01:15:26.440 --> 01:15:29.090
of possession and mediumship

01:15:29.090 --> 01:15:31.723
across the rest of
Mainland Southeast Asia.

01:15:35.398 --> 01:15:39.070
That's a totally different angle

01:15:39.070 --> 01:15:40.437
instead of sort of going out,

01:15:40.437 --> 01:15:43.350
it's even going deeper to
really sort of pay attention

01:15:43.350 --> 01:15:47.370
to local history and society
and culture and politics

01:15:47.370 --> 01:15:48.780
and how it has shaped things

01:15:48.780 --> 01:15:52.730
so that it's not like, all
spirit mediumship looks the same

01:15:52.730 --> 01:15:57.730
from Mandalay to Phnom Penh,
to low Angkor Ban right?

01:16:03.520 --> 01:16:05.580
- Well, this brings us to 1:30.

01:16:05.580 --> 01:16:06.538
So I think I'm gonna bring

01:16:06.538 --> 01:16:09.730
the formal part of our
discussion to a close

01:16:09.730 --> 01:16:10.810
and I'll stop the recording,

01:16:10.810 --> 01:16:15.160
but please join me in a round
of applause for Erick's talk.

01:16:15.160 --> 01:16:17.150
And you're welcome to linger a little bit

01:16:17.150 --> 01:16:19.603
for informal chitchat for a few moments.

